var e={"dermatology-lesion-description":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Morphology** is describing a lesion so precisely that another doctor could redraw it.`,`**Describe first, diagnose second** - the marks are given for the description.`,`Erythroderma means over **90% of body surface** red and scaling - always admit.`,`The pattern name narrows hundreds of skin diseases down to about five.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Primary lesions** come from the disease itself; **secondary lesions** from evolution, scratching or treatment.`,`The **1 cm rule**: macule or patch, papule or plaque or nodule, vesicle or bulla.`,`A **wheal** resolves within 24 hours without leaving a mark.`,`**Petechiae are under 3 mm**; palpable purpura means vasculitis until proved otherwise.`,`An **erosion heals without a scar**; an ulcer reaches the dermis and scars.`,`Patterns: papulosquamous, eczematous, vesiculobullous, urticarial, pustular, granulomatous, pigmentary, ulcerative.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Epidermal turnover takes **28 days**, falling to **3-5 days in psoriasis**, so scale piles up.`,`**Spongiosis** (intercellular oedema) is the hallmark of eczema and gives vesicles and oozing.`,`**Acantholysis** in pemphigus gives a flaccid bulla and a positive Nikolsky sign.`,`Antibodies at the dermoepidermal junction (BP180, BP230) give **tense subepidermal bullae** of pemphigoid.`,`**Interface dermatitis** drops melanin into the dermis, leaving slate-grey pigment incontinence.`,`Histamine on postcapillary venules gives the wheal; deeper oedema gives non-itchy angioedema.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask duration: hours to days suggests urticaria, drug or infection; months suggests chronic disease.`,`Ask **who else itches** - nocturnal itch in the family is scabies until proved otherwise.`,`Take a **drug history for the last 8 weeks**, including ayurvedic and homoeopathic preparations.`,`Ask exactly which creams were used - **OTC steroid combination creams** cause tinea incognito.`,`Ask about pain or numbness - numbness in a patch suggests leprosy.`,`Take a sexual history when palms, soles, genitals or mouth are involved.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine the **whole skin in daylight** after consent, with a chaperone for intimate areas.`,`Never omit scalp, nails, palms, soles, mucosae and web spaces.`,`Use **ten headings**: number, site, size, shape, surface, margin, colour, consistency, arrangement, secondary change.`,`Test **sensation over every hypopigmented patch** with eyes closed; temperature is lost earliest.`,`Palpate peripheral nerves bilaterally: ulnar, radial cutaneous, common peroneal, posterior tibial, great auricular.`,`**Diascopy**: erythema blanches, purpura does not, granuloma shows apple-jelly colour.`,`Close with two positive features, one negative feature and the one test that settles it.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**KOH mount** - 10% for skin, 20-40% for nails - from the active edge before any antifungal.`,`**Wood's lamp (365 nm)**: coral-red erythrasma, golden-yellow pityriasis versicolor, blue-white vitiligo.`,`**Tzanck smear** shows giant cells in herpes and acantholytic cells in pemphigus.`,`Slit-skin smear positive for AFB makes the patient multibacillary regardless of lesion count.`,`Biopsy: **4 mm punch**; blisters need perilesional skin for direct immunofluorescence.`,`Suspected melanoma needs **excision biopsy with 2 mm margin**, never a shave.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**If it is wet, dry it; if dry, wet it** - morphology chooses the vehicle.`,`Oozing lesions get **KMnO4 1:10,000** soaks, pale pink, 10-15 minutes, 3-4 times daily.`,`Apply emollients liberally within 3 minutes of bathing, at least twice a day.`,`Stop irrational steroid-antifungal-antibiotic combination creams and explain why.`,`Measure and photograph lesions with a scale at the first visit.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`One **fingertip unit (0.5 g)** covers two adult palms, about 2% body surface.`,`The patient's own palm with fingers is about **1% of body surface**.`,`Mild steroid on face, flexures and infants; super-potent only on palms, soles, thick plaques.`,`Keep clobetasol **under 50 g a week** in adults to avoid HPA suppression.`,`**Never put a steroid on an undiagnosed annular scaly rash** - do KOH first.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Pain out of proportion, dusky skin or crepitus means **necrotising soft tissue infection**.`,`Nikolsky sign with mucosal erosions and a new drug means **SJS/TEN** - admit today.`,`Palpable purpura with systemic features needs urine analysis and same-day referral.`,`A non-healing ulcer in an old burn scar is a **Marjolin ulcer** - biopsy urgently.`,`Vesicles on the nose tip mean **herpes zoster ophthalmicus** - antiviral and eye referral.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Screen pigmented lesions with **ABCDE** and the ugly duckling sign; evolution is most sensitive.`,`A weighted 7-point checklist score of **3 or more** needs urgent referral.`,`Acral and subungual melanoma is common in Indians; look for **Hutchinson sign**.`,`Review in **2-4 weeks** with the same measurement and photograph.`,`Biopsy any lesion not fitting a pattern after 4-6 weeks of correct treatment.`,`Notify leprosy under NLEP and report adverse drug reactions to **PvPI**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Primary lesions by the 1 cm rule`,columns:[`Content`,`Under 1 cm`,`1 cm or more`,`Typical example`],rows:[[`Flat colour change`,`Macule`,`Patch`,`Vitiligo, leprosy patch`],[`Solid elevation`,`Papule`,`Plaque or nodule`,`Lichen planus, psoriasis`],[`Clear fluid`,`Vesicle`,`Bulla`,`Zoster, pemphigus, pemphigoid`],[`Pus`,`Pustule`,`Abscess`,`Acne, pustular psoriasis`],[`Transient oedema`,`Wheal`,`Giant wheal or angioedema`,`Urticaria`],[`Extravasated blood`,`Petechia (under 3 mm)`,`Ecchymosis`,`Vasculitis, thrombocytopenia`]]},{heading:`Bedside tests and what they show`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`Diascopy`,`Purpura does not blanch; apple-jelly granuloma`,`Red or granulomatous lesions`],[`Nikolsky sign`,`Epidermis shears off normal skin`,`Pemphigus, SJS/TEN, scalded skin`],[`Grattage and Auspitz sign`,`Silvery scale, pinpoint bleeding`,`Suspected psoriasis`],[`Darier sign`,`Rubbed brown lesion forms a wheal`,`Mastocytosis`],[`Dermographism`,`Firm stroke raises a linear wheal`,`Itch without visible rash`],[`Wood's lamp`,`Fluorescence colour by organism; vitiligo blue-white`,`Pigment change, erythrasma, tinea capitis`],[`KOH mount`,`Septate hyphae; spaghetti and meatballs`,`Any scaly annular rash`]]},{heading:`Drugs: topical agents chosen by morphology`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Potassium permanganate 1:10,000`,`Soak 10-15 min, 3-4 times daily`,`2-3 days`,`Stains brown; crystals burn`],[`Calamine lotion`,`Topical, 3-4 times daily`,`As needed`,`Dries already dry skin`],[`Emollient (white soft paraffin)`,`Liberally, at least twice daily`,`Long term`,`Folliculitis; flammable in bulk`],[`Hydrocortisone 1% cream`,`Thin layer once or twice daily`,`Up to 2-4 weeks`,`Minimal; face, flexures, infants`],[`Clobetasol propionate 0.05%`,`Once or twice daily, under 50 g/week`,`Maximum 2-4 weeks`,`Atrophy, rosacea, tinea incognito, HPA suppression`]]}],remember:[`Describe first, diagnose second.`,`Hypopigmented with sensory loss is leprosy; chalk-white with normal sensation is vitiligo.`,`Erosion heals without a scar; ulcer always scars.`,`Flaccid bulla is intraepidermal (pemphigus); tense bulla is subepidermal (pemphigoid).`,`One FTU (0.5 g) covers two adult palms; patient's palm is 1% BSA.`,`ABCDE: Asymmetry, Border, Colour, Diameter over 6 mm, Evolving.`,`Itchy is a symptom; excoriation is the sign.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Describing a skin lesion`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Morphology** = describing a lesion in a fixed vocabulary so precisely that another doctor could redraw it; **describe first, diagnose second** - the marks are for the description.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Primary vs secondary lesions**; **1 cm rule**: macule/patch, papule/plaque or nodule, vesicle/bulla; wheal resolves **within 24 h**; petechiae **under 3 mm**; pattern - papulosquamous, eczematous, vesiculobullous, urticarial, pustular, granulomatous, pigmentary, ulcerative.`],[`Classification - secondary lesions`,`Scale, crust, **erosion (no scar)**, **ulcer (scar)**, excoriation, lichenification, fissure, atrophy, scar, sclerosis; **erythroderma = over 90% BSA** red and scaly - admit.`],[`Pathoanatomy`,`Turnover 28 days -> **3-5 days in psoriasis**; **Dsg1 upper, Dsg3 lower epidermis/mucosa**; spongiosis -> eczema; acantholysis -> **flaccid bulla, Nikolsky**; DEJ (BP180/230, collagen VII) -> **tense subepidermal bulla**; interface dermatitis -> **slate-grey pigment incontinence**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Duration and evolution, first lesion and spread, **itch and who else itches**, pain or numbness, **drugs in last 8 weeks**, **creams used (OTC steroid combinations)**, sun/occupation, atopy, systemic symptoms, diabetes/HIV/pregnancy, sexual history.`],[`Key examination`,`Whole skin in daylight with chaperone; scalp, nails, mucosae, palms, soles, webs; **10 headings**: number, site/distribution, size, shape, surface, margin, colour, consistency, arrangement, secondary change.`],[`Bedside tests`,`**Sensation + nerve palpation** over hypopigmented patches; **diascopy** (purpura does not blanch, apple-jelly granuloma); **Nikolsky**; Darier sign; dermographism; grattage and **Auspitz** in psoriasis.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**KOH 10% skin, 20-40% nails** from active edge; **Wood's lamp 365 nm**; slit-skin smear; **Tzanck** (giant cells, acantholytic cells); **4 mm punch** biopsy + perilesional DIF for blisters; melanoma - **excision biopsy 2 mm margin, never shave**.`],[`Treatment - general`,`**If it is wet, dry it; if dry, wet it**: soaks -> lotion -> cream -> ointment; **KMnO4 1:10,000** pale pink 10-15 min 3-4 times a day; emollients within 3 min of bath; **stop combination creams**; photograph with a scale.`],[`Treatment - topical steroids (FTU)`,`**1 FTU = 0.5 g = two adult palms (2% BSA)**; patient's palm = 1% BSA; mild on face/flexures/infants, super-potent (clobetasol 0.05%) only palms, soles, thick plaques; **under 50 g/week clobetasol**; never on an undiagnosed annular scaly rash.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - pigmented lesions`,`**ABCDE** + ugly duckling; **weighted 7-point checklist** - major 2 points (size, shape, colour change), minor 1 point; **score 3 or more = urgent referral**; acral/subungual melanoma common in Indians, **Hutchinson sign**.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Pain out of proportion/dusky skin/crepitus (necrotising infection), **Nikolsky + mucosa + new drug** (SJS/TEN), palpable purpura with systemic features, erythroderma, Marjolin ulcer, anaesthetic patch (leprosy), zoster at nose tip.`],[`Follow-up`,`Review in **2-4 weeks** with the same measurement and photograph; biopsy any lesion that fails a pattern after 4-6 weeks of correct treatment; notify leprosy (NLEP); report ADRs to **PvPI**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Hypopigmented with sensory loss = leprosy; chalk-white normal sensation = vitiligo; erosion heals without scar, ulcer with scar; flaccid bulla intraepidermal, tense bulla subepidermal; 'itchy' is a symptom, excoriation is the sign.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Describing a lesion at the bedside - exam order`,steps:[{label:`Number, site and distribution`,detail:`Flexural, extensor, photo-exposed, dermatomal, Blaschko, Koebner`},{label:`Size, shape, surface`,detail:`Size in cm (two dimensions); scaly, crusted, umbilicated, verrucous`},{label:`Margin and colour`,detail:`Well or ill defined; active scaly edge, rolled pearly, everted, undermined`},{label:`Consistency, arrangement, secondary change`,detail:`Annular, grouped, linear; erosion, ulcer, lichenification`},{label:`Bedside tests`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Sensation, nerves, diascopy, Nikolsky, Auspitz, Wood's lamp, KOH`},{label:`Name the pattern and two differentials`,tone:`good`,detail:`Two positives, one negative, and the one test that settles it`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Primary lesions by the 1 cm rule`,root:`Primary lesion`,arms:[{label:`Flat`,steps:[`Macule under 1 cm`,`Patch 1 cm or more`,`Vitiligo, leprosy, PV`]},{label:`Solid raised`,steps:[`Papule under 1 cm`,`Plaque or nodule 1 cm or more`,`Psoriasis, lichen planus`]},{label:`Fluid`,steps:[`Vesicle under 1 cm`,`Bulla 1 cm or more`,`Zoster, pemphigus, pemphigoid`]},{label:`Pus or blood`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Pustule, abscess`,`Petechia under 3 mm, ecchymosis`,`Palpable purpura = vasculitis`]}]}]},"dermatology-scabies-pediculosis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Scabies is infestation of the stratum corneum by **Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis**.`,`Spread is by **prolonged skin-to-skin contact** of about 15-20 minutes.`,`The itch is a **type IV hypersensitivity** to the mite, not a bite.`,`An ordinary patient carries only **10-15 mites**; crusted scabies carries thousands to millions.`,`Scabies has been a **WHO neglected tropical disease** since 2017.`]},{title:`Classification and risk factors`,points:[`**IACS 2020**: A confirmed, B clinical, C suspected scabies.`,`Variants: classical, nodular, **crusted (Norwegian)**, incognito, bullous and infantile.`,`Pediculosis: capitis (head), corporis (body, lives in clothing seams), pubis (crab, sexually transmitted).`,`Only the **body louse** transmits typhus, trench fever and relapsing fever.`,`Overcrowding, shared beds, hostels, prisons and old-age homes drive spread.`,`Crusted scabies: **HIV, HTLV-1**, lepromatous leprosy, malnutrition, long-term steroids, or inability to scratch (dementia, paralysis).`,`OTC steroid combination creams cause **scabies incognito**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The female mite burrows about **2 mm a day** and lays 2-3 eggs daily.`,`Larvae hatch in 3-4 days and mature in about 10-14 days.`,`A **first infestation is silent for 4-6 weeks** while sensitisation develops.`,`Re-infestation itches within 1-4 days, so quiet contacts are already infested.`,`Excoriations admit streptococci, causing impetigo and later **post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis**.`,`The mite dies within **2-3 days off the host** and above 50 degrees C.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Nocturnal itch** disturbing sleep is the classic symptom.`,`Ask whether **anyone else at home itches** - the strongest clue.`,`Ask how the previous scabicide was applied, repeated and whether contacts were treated.`,`Ask about creams used, living conditions, sexual contacts and immunosuppression.`,`In a child ask about **puffy eyes and smoky urine** 2-3 weeks after sores.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for the **burrow**: a 3-10 mm grey serpiginous line with a dot at one end.`,`Sites: finger webs, wrists, axillae, waist, buttocks, genitalia; adults spare head and neck.`,`Papules or nodules on the **penis or scrotum** are almost diagnostic.`,`**Infants** have lesions on palms, soles, scalp and face.`,`**Thick warty crusts** with nail dystrophy and little itch mean crusted scabies.`,`Look for impetigo and cellulitis; in children check **BP and urine**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Scabies is a **clinical diagnosis** - treat without waiting for a test.`,`Skin scraping with oil or 10% KOH confirms; a negative excludes nothing.`,`Dermoscopy shows the **delta-wing jet sign**.`,`Crusted scabies needs **HIV, HTLV-1, glucose and CBC**.`,`Child with impetiginised scabies needs a **urine dipstick** and BP.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Permethrin 5% cream** from the jawline down, left on **8-14 hours**.`,`**Repeat on day 7** because the drug does not kill all eggs.`,`Use about **30 g per adult** application; include webs, under nails and genitalia.`,`Apply to face and scalp too in infants, the elderly and immunosuppressed.`,`**Treat all household contacts the same day**, whether or not they itch.`,`Wash clothes and bedding of the last 3 days at **60 degrees C** or seal for 72 hours.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Ivermectin 200 microgram/kg** with a fatty meal, repeated on day 7.`,`Ivermectin suits outbreaks, crusted scabies, failure and unsupervised treatment.`,`**Crusted scabies**: keratolytic, permethrin daily for 7 days, then twice weekly.`,`Add ivermectin on **days 1, 2, 8, 9 and 15** (plus 22 and 29 if severe).`,`**Post-scabetic itch** lasts 2-4 weeks - emollient, mometasone, hydroxyzine, never more scabicide.`,`Nodules need potent steroid or **intralesional triamcinolone**.`,`**Lindane is no longer recommended**; crotamiton is the weakest scabicide.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Permethrin is safe in **pregnancy, lactation** and infants over 2 months.`,`**Sulphur 5-10%** (2-5% infants) is safest under 2 months.`,`Ivermectin is avoided **under 15 kg** and in pregnancy and lactation.`,`Eyelash lice: **white soft paraffin twice daily for 8-10 days**, never a pediculicide.`,`Eyelash lice in a prepubertal child raise suspicion of abuse - follow **POCSO**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **2 and 4 weeks**; new burrows after 2 weeks mean failure or re-infestation.`,`Before calling resistance check **untreated contacts, wrong application, no decontamination**.`,`Refer crusted scabies, cellulitis and suspected nephritis.`,`Institutional outbreaks need mass treatment of everyone on **one day**.`,`Children may return to school after the first full treatment.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for scabies and lice`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Permethrin 5% cream`,`Jawline down, 8-14 h; about 30 g adult`,`Repeat day 7`,`Burning, stinging; age over 2 months`],[`Ivermectin oral`,`200 microgram/kg with fatty meal`,`Repeat day 7`,`Not under 15 kg, pregnancy, lactation`],[`Benzyl benzoate 25%`,`Neck down; 12.5% child, 6.25% infant`,`3 consecutive nights`,`Severe stinging, irritant dermatitis`],[`Sulphur 5-10% ointment`,`Neck down; 2-5% in infants`,`3 consecutive nights`,`Smell, staining; safest under 2 months`],[`Permethrin 1% cream rinse`,`Towel-dried hair, 10 minutes`,`Repeat day 7-9`,`Scalp itch; add wet combing`],[`Dimeticone 4% lotion`,`Head lice, left 8 hours`,`Repeat as for permethrin`,`Physical action, no resistance`],[`Hydroxyzine`,`10-25 mg at night; child 0.5-1 mg/kg/day`,`2-4 weeks`,`Drowsiness; avoid driving`],[`Mometasone 0.1% cream`,`Once daily for post-scabetic eczema`,`1-2 weeks`,`Atrophy if misused`]]},{heading:`IACS 2020 criteria for scabies`,columns:[`Level`,`Criteria (any one)`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`A - Confirmed`,`Mites, eggs or faeces seen on microscopy or dermoscopy`,`Parasitological proof`],[`B - Clinical`,`Burrows, male genital lesions, or typical lesions plus both history features`,`Treat as scabies`],[`C - Suspected`,`Typical lesions plus one history feature`,`Treat if no better diagnosis`],[`History features`,`Itch; close contact who itches`,`Used with B and C`]]},{heading:`Types of pediculosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Head louse`,`Body louse`,`Pubic louse`],rows:[[`Lives in`,`Scalp hair`,`Clothing seams`,`Pubic hair, eyelashes`],[`Key sign`,`Nits within 1 cm of scalp`,`Vagabond pigmentation`,`Maculae ceruleae`],[`Disease spread`,`None`,`Typhus, trench fever, relapsing fever`,`Marker of STI risk`],[`Treatment`,`Permethrin 1% or dimeticone, wet combing`,`Hot wash or discard clothing`,`Permethrin, treat partners`]]}],remember:[`Treat everyone the same day, repeat on day 7, hot-wash bedding.`,`Night itch plus an itching household member is scabies until proved otherwise.`,`Adults are spared above the neck; infants are not.`,`Crusted scabies does not itch and infests the whole ward.`,`Post-scabetic itch lasts 2-4 weeks - do not repeat scabicide.`,`Nits alone do not mean active head lice.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Scabies and pediculosis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Contagious infestation of the stratum corneum by **Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis**, spread by **prolonged skin-to-skin contact**; itch is a **type IV hypersensitivity**; WHO neglected tropical disease since 2017.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IACS 2020**: A confirmed (mite/eggs/faeces or dermoscopy), B clinical (burrows, male genital lesions, or typical lesions + both history features), C suspected; variants - classical, nodular, **crusted (Norwegian)**, incognito, bullous, infantile.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`Overcrowding, shared beds, hostels, prisons, old-age homes; crusted form in **HIV, HTLV-1, LL, malnutrition, Down syndrome, steroids, dementia/paralysis**; OTC steroid creams -> incognito.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Female burrows **2 mm/day**, lays 2-3 eggs/day -> larvae hatch 3-4 days -> adult 10-14 days -> sensitisation (**first infestation silent 4-6 weeks**, re-infestation 1-4 days) -> itch; excoriation -> **Strep pyoderma -> PSGN**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Nocturnal itch + itching household contact**; sites; creams used; how previous scabicide was applied; contacts, living conditions, sexual contacts; immunosuppression; puffy eyes/smoky urine in child.`],[`Key examination`,`**Burrow** 3-10 mm with vesicle/black dot in finger webs, wrists; papules on **penis/scrotum, areolae**; spares head in adults (not infants); nodules; impetigo/ecthyma; **thick warty crusts** with little itch = crusted.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Clinical diagnosis; scraping with oil or 10% KOH (negative excludes nothing); **dermoscopy delta-wing jet**; burrow ink test; crusted - **HIV, HTLV-1, glucose, CBC**; child with pyoderma - **urine dipstick, BP**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Permethrin 5% cream** jawline down (head too in infants, elderly, immunosuppressed), **8-14 h, repeat day 7**, about **30 g adult**; age over 2 months, pregnancy and lactation; **treat all household contacts the same day**.`],[`Treatment - oral ivermectin`,`**200 microgram/kg with a fatty meal, repeat day 7** (12 mg for 50-65 kg); outbreaks, crusted, failure, unsupervised; **not under 15 kg, pregnancy, lactation**; not ovicidal.`],[`Treatment - alternative scabicides`,`**Benzyl benzoate 25%** 3 nights (12.5% child, 6.25% infant); **sulphur 5-10%** (2-5% infant) 3 nights - safest under 2 months; crotamiton weakest; **lindane no longer recommended**.`],[`Treatment - crusted scabies`,`Keratolytic (salicylic acid 5-10%, urea 10-20%) + **permethrin daily 7 days then twice weekly** + **ivermectin days 1, 2, 8, 9, 15 (+22, 29)**; barrier nursing; seal items 7 days.`],[`Treatment - itch, nodules, pyoderma`,`**Post-scabetic itch 2-4 weeks** - emollient, mometasone 0.1% 1-2 weeks, hydroxyzine at night - never more scabicide; nodules - potent steroid or **intralesional triamcinolone**; impetigo - mupirocin/fusidic acid, extensive - cefalexin 500 mg 6-hourly 7 days.`],[`Treatment - pediculosis`,`Head lice: **permethrin 1% 10 min, repeat day 7-9**, dimeticone 4%, wet combing; oral ivermectin day 1 and 8 if resistant; body lice: **hot-wash clothing**; pubic lice: treat partners, **eyelashes - paraffin twice daily 8-10 days**.`],[`Prevention - decontamination`,`Wash clothes and bedding of last 3 days at **60 C**, or **seal 72 hours**; fumigation unnecessary (mite dies in 2-3 days off host); children back to school after first full treatment.`],[`Complications`,`Impetigo, ecthyma, cellulitis, **post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis**, rheumatic fever, eczematisation, irritant dermatitis from overuse; body lice transmit **typhus, trench fever, relapsing fever**.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Crusted scabies, cellulitis, nephritis features in a child, institutional outbreak (mass treatment on one day), bullous scabies in elderly, **eyelash lice in a child - POCSO**.`],[`Follow-up`,`Review at **2 and 4 weeks**; new burrows after 2 weeks = failure or re-infestation - check **untreated contacts, wrong application, no decontamination** before calling resistance.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Treat everyone the same day, repeat day 7, hot-wash; adults spared above neck, infants are not; crusted scabies does not itch and infests the ward; nits alone do not mean active head lice.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Itchy patient - is it scabies and what next?`,steps:[{label:`Night itch + itching household contact?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Plus papules in webs, wrists, waist, genitals - IACS clinical/suspected`},{label:`Look for burrow, genital papules, nodules`,detail:`Dermoscopy delta-wing jet if available; scraping optional`},{label:`Thick crusts, little itch, immunosuppressed?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Crusted scabies - isolate, HIV test, combined ivermectin + topical`},{label:`Permethrin 5% neck down, repeat day 7`,detail:`30 g adult; head too in infants, elderly; ivermectin 200 mcg/kg if outbreak`},{label:`Treat all contacts same day, hot-wash bedding`,detail:`Contacts of last 6 weeks; seal unwashables 72 h`},{label:`Review 2 and 4 weeks`,tone:`good`,detail:`Post-scabetic itch up to 4 weeks is expected - steroid + emollient`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Choosing the scabicide by patient`,steps:[{label:`Infant under 2 months`,detail:`Precipitated sulphur 2-5% on 3 consecutive nights`},{label:`Child, adult, pregnancy, lactation`,detail:`Permethrin 5% 8-14 h, repeat day 7`},{label:`Cheap alternative`,detail:`Benzyl benzoate 25% (12.5% child) on 3 nights`},{label:`Outbreak, failure, unsupervised`,detail:`Ivermectin 200 mcg/kg with food, repeat day 7; not under 15 kg or pregnancy`},{label:`Crusted scabies`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Keratolytic + daily permethrin + ivermectin days 1, 2, 8, 9, 15`}]}]},"dermatology-eczema-atopic-dermatitis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Eczema and dermatitis are synonyms**; the histological hallmark is **spongiosis**.`,`**Itch is the defining symptom** of every eczema.`,`Atopic dermatitis starts in the first year in 60% and before 5 years in 85%.`,`About two-thirds of children clear by adolescence.`,`Diagnose with the **UK Working Party criteria**: itch plus 3 or more of 5.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Endogenous**: atopic, seborrhoeic, nummular, asteatotic, stasis, pompholyx, lichen simplex chronicus.`,`**Exogenous**: irritant contact, allergic contact, photo-contact and infective eczema.`,`**Acute** eczema oozes with papulovesicles; subacute scales; **chronic** shows lichenification.`,`The stage chooses the vehicle, not only the drug.`,`Severity is judged by extent, intensity and **impact on sleep and school**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Filaggrin loss** and low ceramides raise water loss, causing dry skin.`,`The leaky barrier lets allergens, irritants and Staphylococcus aureus in.`,`Alarmins (**TSLP, IL-33, IL-25**) from injured keratinocytes start the type 2 response.`,`**IL-4 and IL-13** further suppress filaggrin and drive IgE - a vicious cycle.`,`**IL-31** acts directly on itch nerves, so atopic itch is largely non-histaminergic.`,`Scratching thickens skin into **lichenification**, completing the itch-scratch cycle.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask age of onset: early childhood suggests atopy; adult onset suggests contact, scabies or lymphoma.`,`Ask about **sleep loss and school** - disability drives escalation more than area.`,`Ask personal and family history of asthma, rhinitis and food allergy.`,`Take a contact history: **hair dye (PPD)**, cement, nickel, footwear, bindi, parthenium.`,`Ask **how much emollient and steroid** is actually used - undertreatment is common.`,`Ask about sudden painful weeping with fever - think **eczema herpeticum**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Eczema has an **ill-defined margin**; psoriasis is sharp, tinea has a raised edge.`,`Infants: cheeks, scalp and extensors with **napkin sparing**.`,`Children: **antecubital and popliteal fossae**, wrists, ankles and neck.`,`Atopic stigmata: **Dennie-Morgan folds**, palmar hyperlinearity, pityriasis alba, keratosis pilaris.`,`Look for infection, steroid damage, and burrows of missed scabies.`,`Plot height and weight in children with severe disease.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Atopic dermatitis is a **clinical diagnosis** - no routine blood test.`,`**KOH** any annular or unilateral plaque to exclude tinea incognito.`,`**Patch testing** (read at 48 and 96 hours) for chronic hand, face or foot eczema.`,`Food IgE tests only with a clear **immediate reaction** history.`,`Biopsy refractory adult eczema to exclude **mycosis fungoides**.`,`Before systemic therapy: CBC, LFT, creatinine, BP, hepatitis, HIV and TB screen.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Emollients** at least twice daily: **250-500 g/week adults**, 150-250 g/week children.`,`Use a soap substitute and apply emollient within 3 minutes of bathing.`,`Topical steroid **once daily** to inflamed skin until **48 hours after** the flare settles.`,`Mild steroid on face and flexures; potent on trunk, limbs and lichenified skin.`,`**Proactive twice-weekly** steroid or tacrolimus on old sites prevents relapse.`,`**Tacrolimus or pimecrolimus** for face, eyelids and flexures - no atrophy.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Newer topicals: **roflumilast 0.15%** and **tapinarof 1%** once daily, strongly recommended in adults.`,`**Narrowband UVB** 2-3 times weekly for extensive moderate-severe disease.`,`**Ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day** is the fastest systemic agent.`,`Methotrexate, azathioprine (after TPMT) and **dupilumab** are steroid-sparing options.`,`Lebrikizumab, tralokinumab and **nemolizumab** are now strongly recommended in adults.`,`**Oral JAK inhibitors** stop itch within days; avoid in over-65s, smokers and thrombosis risk.`,`**Oral steroids are not maintenance** - at most a 3-7 day course.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`In **pregnancy**, emollients and mild-to-moderate steroids are safe.`,`**Ciclosporin** is the systemic drug of choice in pregnancy.`,`Methotrexate and JAK inhibitors are **contraindicated in pregnancy**.`,`Vaccines are not contraindicated in eczema; avoid live vaccines on immunosuppressants.`,`Indian skin shows more follicular papules, lichenification and post-inflammatory pigmentation.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Eczema herpeticum**: punched-out erosions with fever - start aciclovir the same day.`,`Golden crusts and weeping mean **S. aureus** infection.`,`Over 90% of skin red and scaling is **erythroderma** - admit.`,`Recurrent deep infections or failure to thrive suggest **primary immunodeficiency** - refer children with faltering growth.`,`Atrophy and perioral dermatitis follow months of OTC combination creams.`,`Review **2-4 weeks** after starting; check grams per week and technique; ciclosporin needs BP and creatinine **every 2 weeks**.`,`Refer if **1-2 weeks of potent steroid** fails, systemic therapy is needed, or contact allergy needs patch tests.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for eczema`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Emollient`,`Twice daily; 250-500 g/week adult`,`Indefinitely`,`Folliculitis; flammable on fabric`],[`Hydrocortisone 1% cream`,`Once daily to face, flexures, infants`,`Until 48 h after clearing`,`Minimal`],[`Mometasone furoate 0.1%`,`Once daily to trunk and limbs`,`1-2 weeks, then twice weekly`,`Atrophy, striae, tinea incognito`],[`Clobetasol propionate 0.05%`,`Once daily; max 50 g/week`,`Up to 2 weeks`,`Never face, flexures or children`],[`Tacrolimus 0.03% or 0.1% ointment`,`Twice daily; 0.03% from 2 y, 0.1% from 16 y`,`Then twice weekly`,`Burning first week; not on infected skin`],[`Ciclosporin oral`,`3-5 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`Up to 1 year`,`Hypertension, nephrotoxicity; BP and creatinine`],[`Methotrexate oral or SC`,`7.5-25 mg once weekly plus folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`Onset 8-12 weeks`,`Cytopenia, hepatotoxicity, teratogenic`],[`Dupilumab SC`,`600 mg loading, then 300 mg every 2 weeks`,`Long term`,`Conjunctivitis, injection-site reaction`],[`Hydroxyzine oral`,`10-25 mg at night; child 0.5-1 mg/kg/day`,`1-2 weeks in flares`,`Drowsiness, QT prolongation`],[`Aciclovir (eczema herpeticum)`,`400 mg five times daily; IV 5-10 mg/kg 8-hourly`,`7-10 days`,`Start same day; refer if periocular`]]},{heading:`Topical steroid potency and where to use`,columns:[`Potency`,`Example`,`Where to use`,`Maximum duration`],rows:[[`Mild`,`Hydrocortisone 1%`,`Face, eyelids, flexures, infants`,`Weeks, repeat for flares`],[`Moderate`,`Clobetasone butyrate 0.05%`,`Trunk and limbs, children and adults`,`2-4 weeks, then proactive`],[`Potent`,`Mometasone 0.1%, betamethasone valerate 0.1%`,`Adult trunk, limbs, lichenified plaques`,`Up to 2-4 weeks`],[`Very potent`,`Clobetasol propionate 0.05%`,`Palms, soles, thick plaques`,`2 weeks; never on face`],[`Steroid-sparing`,`Tacrolimus, pimecrolimus`,`Face, eyelids, maintenance`,`Long-term twice weekly`]]},{heading:`UK Working Party criteria for atopic dermatitis`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Essential`,`An itchy skin condition`],[`1 - Flexural history`,`Skin crease involvement (cheeks if under 10)`],[`2 - Atopy`,`Personal asthma or hay fever (relative if under 4)`],[`3 - Dry skin`,`Generally dry skin in the past year`],[`4 - Visible flexural eczema`,`Seen on examination`],[`5 - Early onset`,`Before age 2 (not used if under 4)`],[`Diagnosis`,`Essential plus 3 or more of 1 to 5`]]}],remember:[`The margin decides: eczema ill defined, psoriasis sharp, tinea raised active edge.`,`Emollient in grams per week; steroid in fingertip units.`,`Mild on face, potent on body, very potent only on palms and soles.`,`Proactive twice-weekly therapy halves relapses.`,`Atopic itch is IL-31 driven - non-sedating antihistamines do little.`,`Systemic steroids buy a week and cost a rebound.`,`Punched-out erosions with fever mean eczema herpeticum.`]},updated:[`NICE CG57 (amended September 2025): once-daily bathing or showering is fine; bathing less often, ion-exchange water softeners and silk clothing give no benefit`,`AAD 2025 focused update: roflumilast 0.15% cream and tapinarof 1% cream added as strongly recommended non-steroid topicals for adult AD (crisaborole remains an option)`,`AAD 2025 focused update: lebrikizumab (anti-IL-13) and nemolizumab (anti-IL-31 receptor, with topicals) strongly recommended for moderate-severe adult AD alongside dupilumab and tralokinumab`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Eczema and atopic dermatitis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Eczema = dermatitis: itchy **spongiotic** epidermal inflammation; atopic dermatitis = chronic relapsing itchy eczema, **60% onset in first year, 85% before 5 years**, two-thirds clear by adolescence.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Endogenous** (atopic, seborrhoeic, nummular, asteatotic, stasis, pompholyx, LSC) vs **exogenous** (irritant, allergic contact, photocontact, infective); stage **acute (ooze) / subacute (scale) / chronic (lichenified)** chooses the vehicle; severity mild/moderate/severe by impact.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**UK Working Party**: itchy skin + **3 or more of** flexural involvement, atopy history, dry skin, visible flexural eczema, onset before 2 years; Hanifin-Rajka for research.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Filaggrin loss** -> barrier leak, TEWL -> alarmins (TSLP, IL-33, IL-25) -> **Th2: IL-4, IL-13, IL-31** -> itch (non-histaminergic) -> scratch -> lichenification; S. aureus colonises over 90%; HSV spreads (eczema herpeticum).`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Onset age, sleep loss, atopy, triggers (soap, heat, wool), **contact/occupational** (PPD dye, cement, nickel, parthenium), **all creams used**, quantities used, food reactions (only immediate ones matter), growth, itching contacts (scabies).`],[`Key examination`,`**Ill-defined margin**; infant cheeks/extensors with napkin sparing, child **flexures**, adult hands/face; Dennie-Morgan folds, hyperlinearity, pityriasis alba; infection; steroid damage; burrows; KOH any annular plaque; BSA by palm.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Clinical; **KOH** for tinea incognito, scraping for scabies; swab only if resistant; **Tzanck/HSV PCR** for herpeticum (do not wait); **patch test** for hand, face, eyelid, adult-onset; no routine IgE; pre-systemic CBC, LFT, creatinine, HBV, HCV, HIV, TB.`],[`Treatment - general`,`Written action plan; **once-daily lukewarm bath** with soap substitute, emollient within 3 min; **no benefit from less bathing, water softeners or silk clothing** (NICE 2025); cotton, short nails; no elimination diets without proven IgE allergy; tackle steroid phobia.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Emollient 250-500 g/week adult, 150-250 g/week child**, smoothed downwards; **topical steroid once daily** by site (mild face/flexures, potent trunk/limbs) until **48 h after clearing**; then **twice-weekly proactive** steroid or tacrolimus up to 16 weeks.`],[`Treatment - non-steroid topicals`,`**Tacrolimus 0.03% (2 years and over) or 0.1% (16 and over)** twice daily for face, eyelids, flexures (burning first week); **roflumilast 0.15% cream** and **tapinarof 1% cream** once daily now strongly recommended (AAD 2025); crisaborole 2% from 3 months.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`NB-UVB; **ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day** (fastest; BP, creatinine 2-weekly); **methotrexate 7.5-25 mg weekly + folic acid**; azathioprine after TPMT; **dupilumab 600 mg then 300 mg every 2 weeks**; lebrikizumab, tralokinumab, nemolizumab; JAK inhibitors (upadacitinib, abrocitinib, baricitinib).`],[`FTU and how to apply`,`**1 FTU = 0.5 g = two adult palms**; adult whole body about 40 FTU (20 g); emollient first, steroid **30 min later**; ointment for dry/lichenified, cream for moist/face; wet wraps short term in children.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: emollient, mild-moderate steroid, ciclosporin if systemic needed - **no methotrexate or JAK inhibitor**; children: growth monitoring with potent steroids; elderly/smokers/CV risk: avoid JAK inhibitors unless no alternative; no live vaccines on immunosuppressants.`],[`Complications`,`Impetiginisation, **eczema herpeticum**, molluscum, erythroderma, steroid atrophy/rosacea/HPA suppression, sleep and growth loss, atopic keratoconjunctivitis, cataract; **dupilumab conjunctivitis**.`],[`Treatment - infected eczema, herpeticum`,`Localised: fusidic acid or mupirocin 2% three times daily 5-7 days; widespread: **cloxacillin or cefalexin 500 mg 6-hourly 7 days**; herpeticum: **aciclovir 400 mg five times daily 7-10 days** (child 20 mg/kg four times daily), IV 5-10 mg/kg 8-hourly if unwell - start today.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Punched-out erosions with fever (herpeticum - same day, eye referral if periocular), erythroderma, cellulitis, failure of 1-2 weeks of potent steroid, need for systemic therapy or patch tests, faltering growth, adult refractory eczema with poikiloderma (mycosis fungoides).`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **2-4 weeks**: grams used, FTU technique, sleep, infection; ciclosporin BP and creatinine 2-weekly for 3 months; methotrexate/azathioprine CBC and LFT; eyelid steroid users - eye checks.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AAD 2025**: roflumilast, tapinarof, lebrikizumab, nemolizumab strongly recommended (adults); **NICE CG57 2025**: once-daily bathing fine, no water softeners or silk clothing.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Eczema margin ill defined, psoriasis sharp, tinea active edge; emollient in grams/week, steroid in FTU; atopic itch is IL-31 driven - non-sedating antihistamines do little; systemic steroids buy a week and cost a rebound.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Atopic dermatitis treatment ladder (AAD 2025, NICE)`,steps:[{label:`Emollients + soap substitute for all`,detail:`250-500 g/week adult; once-daily bath; avoid triggers`},{label:`Topical steroid by site for flares`,detail:`Once daily until 48 h after clear; mild face, potent trunk`},{label:`Proactive twice-weekly maintenance`,detail:`Moderate steroid or tacrolimus to old sites up to 16 weeks`},{label:`Non-steroid topicals`,detail:`Tacrolimus/pimecrolimus; roflumilast 0.15% or tapinarof 1% once daily`},{label:`Phototherapy or conventional systemic`,detail:`NB-UVB; ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day; methotrexate weekly; azathioprine`},{label:`Biologic or JAK inhibitor`,tone:`good`,detail:`Dupilumab, lebrikizumab, tralokinumab, nemolizumab; upadacitinib, abrocitinib`},{label:`Never maintenance oral steroid`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Prednisolone 3-7 days only as bridge - rebound on stopping`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Eczema suddenly worse - work it out`,steps:[{label:`Punched-out erosions, fever, pain?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Eczema herpeticum - aciclovir today, eye referral if periocular`},{label:`Golden crusts, weeping?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`S. aureus - fusidic acid local; cefalexin 500 mg 6-hourly if widespread`},{label:`Annular edge or household itch?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`KOH for tinea incognito; look for burrows (scabies)`},{label:`Site pattern of a contact?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Patch test - hair dye, cement, nickel, footwear, parthenium`},{label:`Check adherence and grams used`,detail:`Undertreatment from steroid phobia is commonest`},{label:`Escalate ladder if still uncontrolled`,tone:`good`,detail:`Phototherapy, ciclosporin, biologic`}]}]},"dermatology-acne-vulgaris":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Acne is chronic inflammation of the **pilosebaceous unit** on face, chest and back.`,`It affects **over 80% of adolescents** and often persists in adult women.`,`**No comedone, no acne** - the comedone separates acne from its mimics.`,`Record **scarring and psychological impact** separately; either justifies stepping up.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**IADVL grading**: Grade 1 comedonal, Grade 2 papular, Grade 3 pustular, Grade 4 nodulocystic.`,`The grade, not the photograph, decides topical, oral or isotretinoin therapy.`,`**Acne conglobata** has interconnecting nodules and sinuses.`,`**Acne fulminans** is acute nodular acne with fever, arthralgia and bone lesions.`,`Other variants: adult female hormonal, mechanica, pomade, cosmetic and drug-induced acne.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Step 1: **follicular hyperkeratinisation** forms the microcomedone - the target of retinoids.`,`Step 2: **androgen-driven sebum excess** via dihydrotestosterone on sebocytes.`,`Step 3: **Cutibacterium acnes** splits sebum into irritant free fatty acids.`,`Step 4: **inflammation** through TLR-2 and IL-1 beta gives papules and pustules.`,`Follicle rupture into the dermis gives deep nodules that scar.`,`In Indian skin, **post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation** often outlasts the acne.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask age of onset; acne after 25 in a woman suggests hormonal acne.`,`Ask about deep painful lumps, sinuses and new scars - these mean isotretinoin.`,`Ask exactly what was used and how - wrong use is not drug failure.`,`Ask about **OTC steroid and fairness creams**, gym supplements and drugs.`,`In women ask about irregular periods, hirsutism and weight gain - **PCOS**.`,`Ask about **mood and suicidal thoughts** and pregnancy plans.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine face, neck, chest, shoulders and back in good light.`,`Stretch the skin to find **closed comedones** - the lesions that rupture.`,`**Nodules are over 5 mm**, deep, tender and scarring.`,`Record scar type: ice-pick, boxcar, rolling, hypertrophic or keloidal.`,`Look for hyperandrogenism: hirsutism, alopecia, acanthosis nigricans, virilisation.`,`Exclude mimics: rosacea, perioral dermatitis, Malassezia folliculitis, steroid acne.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Ordinary acne needs no investigation.**`,`Suspected PCOS: testosterone, DHEAS, LH, FSH, prolactin, TSH, 17-hydroxyprogesterone, pelvic ultrasound.`,`Very high androgens with rapid virilisation suggest a **tumour** - refer urgently.`,`Before isotretinoin: **fasting lipids, ALT and pregnancy test**.`,`Repeat lipids and ALT at **4-8 weeks**; pregnancy test **monthly**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Topical retinoid is the backbone** for every grade; adapalene 0.1% is first choice.`,`Apply a **pea-sized amount** to the whole face at night, 20 minutes after washing.`,`**Benzoyl peroxide 2.5-5%** kills C. acnes with no resistance.`,`**Adapalene plus benzoyl peroxide** fixed combination is first line for Grades 2-3.`,`**Topical clindamycin is never used alone** - always with benzoyl peroxide.`,`Judge any regimen only after **8-12 weeks**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Doxycycline 100 mg daily for 8-12 weeks**, maximum 3-4 months, with retinoid and BPO.`,`Take doxycycline with water and food, upright, away from iron, calcium and milk.`,`Women with hormonal acne: **combined pill** with drospirenone or cyproterone, or **spironolactone**.`,`**Isotretinoin** for nodulocystic, scarring or resistant acne, or severe distress.`,`Isotretinoin dose: 0.5 mg/kg/day, then up to 1 mg/kg/day, to **120-150 mg/kg cumulative**.`,`Take isotretinoin **with a fatty meal**; low dose 0.25-0.4 mg/kg/day is also effective.`,`**Maintenance retinoid for 6-12 months** after clearance prevents relapse.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen** - two contraceptives from 1 month before to 1 month after.`,`In pregnancy use azelaic acid, benzoyl peroxide, clindamycin with BPO, or azithromycin.`,`All retinoids, tetracyclines and spironolactone are **avoided in pregnancy**.`,`Tetracyclines are contraindicated **under 8 years** because of tooth staining.`,`In PCOS, **5-10% weight loss** improves acne.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Acne fulminans**: prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day first, then low-dose isotretinoin.`,`Triglycerides **over 500 mg/dL** on isotretinoin - stop it; pancreatitis risk.`,`**Isotretinoin plus tetracycline** causes benign intracranial hypertension - never combine.`,`Suicidal thoughts need urgent mental health assessment.`,`Review at **8-12 weeks** with photographs, counting new inflammatory lesions; stop oral antibiotics by **3-4 months** whether or not clear.`,`On isotretinoin review **monthly** (pregnancy test, mood, side effects, cumulative dose); a missed period means stop and test that day.`,`Refer for isotretinoin, fulminans, conglobata, failure of two courses and scar revision.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for acne`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Adapalene 0.1% gel`,`Pea-size to whole face at night`,`Months; maintenance 6-12 months`,`Dryness, peeling, early flare; not in pregnancy`],[`Benzoyl peroxide 2.5-5%`,`Topical once or twice daily`,`Long term`,`Irritation, bleaches hair and fabric`],[`Clindamycin 1% gel`,`Once or twice daily, only with BPO`,`Up to 12 weeks`,`Resistance if used alone`],[`Azelaic acid 15-20%`,`Topical twice daily`,`Months`,`Mild burning; safe in pregnancy`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg once daily orally`,`8-12 weeks, max 3-4 months`,`Photosensitivity, oesophagitis; not pregnancy or under 8`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg three times weekly orally`,`8-12 weeks`,`GI upset, QT prolongation`],[`Combined pill (drospirenone or cyproterone)`,`Daily orally`,`Judge at 3-6 months`,`VTE; stop cyproterone 3 months after control`],[`Spironolactone`,`50-100 mg daily (25-200 mg)`,`Months`,`Menstrual irregularity; contraception needed; women only`],[`Isotretinoin`,`0.5 then up to 1 mg/kg/day with fatty meal`,`To 120-150 mg/kg, 6-8 months`,`Teratogen, cheilitis, raised lipids and ALT, mood`],[`Prednisolone (acne fulminans)`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day orally`,`About 4 weeks, then taper`,`Start before low-dose isotretinoin`]]},{heading:`IADVL acne grading and treatment ladder`,columns:[`Grade`,`Lesions`,`First line`,`Add-on or alternative`],rows:[[`Grade 1`,`Comedones only`,`Topical retinoid at night`,`BPO, azelaic or salicylic acid`],[`Grade 2`,`Papules with comedones`,`Adapalene plus BPO combination`,`Topical clindamycin with BPO`],[`Grade 3`,`Pustules, often truncal`,`Doxycycline 8-12 weeks plus retinoid and BPO`,`Azithromycin; pill or spironolactone`],[`Grade 4`,`Nodulocystic or scarring`,`Isotretinoin to 120-150 mg/kg`,`Prednisolone first in fulminans`],[`After clearance`,`Clear or almost clear`,`Retinoid maintenance 6-12 months`,`Add BPO if lesions recur`]]},{heading:`Acne versus its mimics`,columns:[`Feature`,`Acne`,`Rosacea`,`Steroid acne`,`Malassezia folliculitis`],rows:[[`Comedones`,`Present`,`Absent`,`Absent`,`Absent`],[`Lesions`,`Polymorphic`,`Papules on central erythema`,`Monomorphic pustules`,`Itchy monomorphic papules`],[`Site`,`Face, chest, back`,`Central face`,`Where steroid applied`,`Upper trunk`],[`Clue`,`Starts at puberty`,`Telangiectasia, flushing`,`Steroid cream history`,`KOH shows yeasts`]]}],remember:[`No comedone, no acne.`,`Four steps, four drug groups: plug, sebum, C. acnes, inflammation.`,`Benzoyl peroxide must accompany every antibiotic; topical antibiotics never alone.`,`Doxycycline maximum 3-4 months, always with retinoid and BPO.`,`Isotretinoin with a fatty meal to 120-150 mg/kg; cheilitis proves adherence.`,`Never combine isotretinoin with a tetracycline.`,`Treat the acne to treat the pigmentation.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Acne vulgaris`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Chronic inflammatory disease of the **pilosebaceous unit** with comedones, papules, pustules, nodules and cysts on face, chest, back; **no comedone, no acne**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IADVL grade 1 comedonal, 2 papular, 3 pustular, 4 nodulocystic**; record **scarring and psychological impact** separately - either justifies stepping up; variants - conglobata, **fulminans**, adult female, mechanica, drug-induced.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`Puberty/androgens, family history, PCOS, high glycaemic diet and skimmed milk/whey (modest), occlusive hair oils, cosmetics, masks; drugs - **steroids, anabolic steroids, lithium, phenytoin, isoniazid, EGFR inhibitors**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Follicular hyperkeratinisation -> microcomedone -> androgen-driven sebum -> C. acnes lipases -> TLR2/NLRP3, IL-1 beta -> papule/pustule -> rupture -> nodule -> scar/PIH**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Onset age, sites (truncal = severe), **painful nodules, sinuses, scars**, everything used and how, OTC steroid/fairness creams, drugs, **menstrual irregularity, hirsutism**, mood and suicidality, pregnancy plans.`],[`Key examination`,`Count comedones (open/closed), papules, pustules, **nodules over 5 mm**; scar type (ice-pick, boxcar, rolling, keloid); PIH; hyperandrogenism signs; mimics - rosacea, perioral dermatitis, **Malassezia folliculitis**, steroid acne, gram-negative folliculitis.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`None for ordinary acne; women with hyperandrogenism - **testosterone, DHEAS, LH/FSH, prolactin, TSH, 17-OHP**, pelvic USG; pre-isotretinoin **fasting lipids, ALT, pregnancy test**, repeat at **4-8 weeks**; no routine K+ on spironolactone.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Topical retinoid for every grade** (adapalene 0.1% pea-size at night); **BPO 2.5-5%** (no resistance); grade 2-3 **adapalene + BPO fixed combination**; topical clindamycin **only with BPO**; azelaic acid 15-20% (pregnancy, PIH).`],[`Escalation / second line`,`Grade 3/truncal: **doxycycline 100 mg daily 8-12 weeks (max 3-4 months)** with retinoid + BPO; azithromycin if tetracycline contraindicated; women - **COC (drospirenone/cyproterone)** or **spironolactone 50-100 mg**; clascoterone 1%.`],[`Treatment - isotretinoin`,`Grade 4, scarring, resistant, severe distress: **0.5 mg/kg/day then up to 1 mg/kg/day to 120-150 mg/kg cumulative** (or low dose 0.25-0.4 mg/kg); **with fatty meal**; **2 contraceptives 1 month before to 1 month after, monthly pregnancy test**; never with tetracycline.`],[`How to use`,`Retinoid 20 min after washing, pea-size for whole face, alternate nights initially; BPO morning if tretinoin at night; doxycycline upright with water and food, away from iron/calcium/milk, sunscreen.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: azelaic acid, BPO, clindamycin + BPO, erythromycin/azithromycin; **avoid all retinoids, tetracyclines, spironolactone**; under 8 years no tetracycline; PCOS - weight loss 5-10%.`],[`Complications`,`Scarring, keloids, PIH, psychological harm; isotretinoin - cheilitis, dry eyes, **TG over 500 mg/dL (stop - pancreatitis)**, ALT over 3x, benign intracranial hypertension, teratogenicity, mood change.`],[`Treatment - acne fulminans`,`Fever, arthralgia, ulcerated nodules, bone lesions -> **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day about 4 weeks first**, then low-dose isotretinoin 0.1-0.25 mg/kg/day.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Nodules/sinuses/early scars (isotretinoin), fulminans, **virilisation (tumour)**, missed period on isotretinoin, headache with blurred vision, suicidal ideation, failure of two adequate courses.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Judge at **8-12 weeks** by new inflammatory lesion count; stop oral antibiotics by 3-4 months; isotretinoin monthly review; **maintenance topical retinoid 6-12 months** after clearance.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AAD 2024**: strong recommendations for BPO, topical retinoids, fixed-dose combinations, doxycycline and **isotretinoin** (low-dose acceptable); **clascoterone 1%** and sarecycline added; isotretinoin needs only lipids/ALT - **no routine CBC**; limit oral antibiotics to 3-4 months.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Four steps, four drug groups; BPO with every antibiotic; doxycycline max 12 weeks; isotretinoin with fatty meal to 120-150 mg/kg; cheilitis proves adherence; treat the acne to treat the pigment.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Acne ladder by grade`,steps:[{label:`Grade 1 - comedonal`,detail:`Adapalene 0.1% or tretinoin nightly; add BPO or azelaic acid`},{label:`Grade 2 - papular`,detail:`Adapalene + BPO 2.5% fixed combination at night`},{label:`Grade 3 - pustular or truncal`,detail:`Doxycycline 100 mg daily 8-12 weeks + retinoid + BPO; COC or spironolactone in women`},{label:`Grade 4 - nodulocystic or scarring`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Isotretinoin 0.5-1 mg/kg/day to 120-150 mg/kg; pregnancy prevention`},{label:`Maintenance after clearance`,tone:`good`,detail:`Topical retinoid with or without BPO for 6-12 months`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Isotretinoin course - safe sequence`,steps:[{label:`Confirm indication and record mood`,detail:`Nodular, scarring, resistant, severe distress`},{label:`Baseline lipids, ALT, pregnancy test`,detail:`Two contraceptives from 1 month before`},{label:`Start 0.5 mg/kg/day with a fatty meal`,detail:`Lip balm, emollient, artificial tears; no tetracycline, no vitamin A`},{label:`Repeat lipids and ALT at 4-8 weeks`,tone:`decision`,detail:`TG over 500 mg/dL stop; ALT over 3x reduce`},{label:`Monthly review: pregnancy test, mood`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Suicidal ideation or headache with blurred vision - stop and refer`},{label:`Reach 120-150 mg/kg cumulative`,tone:`good`,detail:`Contraception 1 month after; then retinoid maintenance`}]}]},"dermatology-urticaria-angioedema":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **wheal** is an itchy oedematous swelling that **resolves within 24 hours** leaving normal skin.`,`**Angioedema** is deeper oedema - tingling or painful, not itchy - lasting **up to 72 hours**.`,`About **20% of people** have urticaria at some time; CSU affects about 1%.`,`About half of chronic spontaneous urticaria resolves within **1-2 years**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Acute urticaria** lasts 6 weeks or less; **chronic** lasts over 6 weeks.`,`Chronic is **spontaneous (CSU)** or **inducible (CIndU)** with a physical trigger.`,`**Dermographism** is the commonest inducible type; others are cold, pressure, cholinergic, solar.`,`Angioedema is **histaminergic** (with wheals) or **bradykinin-mediated** (without wheals or itch).`,`Activity is scored by **UAS7 (0-42)**; 16 or more on 4-fold antihistamine suggests omalizumab.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Mast cell degranulation** is the final common pathway, releasing histamine and leukotrienes.`,`Histamine on H1 receptors leaks plasma into the superficial dermis, forming the wheal.`,`In CSU, **IgE autoantibodies** (anti-TPO, IL-24) or IgG against the IgE receptor activate mast cells.`,`**NSAIDs** worsen up to 30% of CSU by shunting arachidonic acid to leukotrienes.`,`**ACE inhibitors** raise bradykinin; in HAE, low C1 inhibitor lets bradykinin rise.`,`Bradykinin acts on **B2 receptors** with no mast cell involvement, so no itch or wheal.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **how long one wheal lasts** - over 24 hours suggests urticarial vasculitis.`,`Ask about tongue or throat swelling, voice change and breathing difficulty.`,`Ask about food, drug or sting in the 2 hours before an acute episode.`,`Take a drug history: **NSAIDs, codeine, ACE inhibitors**, oestrogen pills.`,`Ask about angioedema without wheals, abdominal attacks and family history - **HAE**.`,`Check how antihistamines were taken - on-demand use explains most failure.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Wheals are well demarcated with **normal skin between - no scale or scar**.`,`Patients **rub rather than scratch**, so excoriations suggest another diagnosis.`,`Stroke the back firmly - a linear wheal in 5-10 minutes is **dermographism**.`,`Check lips, tongue, stridor, wheeze, SpO2 and BP to exclude **anaphylaxis**.`,`Residual purpura or pigmentation points to **urticarial vasculitis**.`,`Ice cube test for 5 minutes confirms **cold urticaria**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Acute urticaria needs no routine tests.**`,`CSU needs only **CBC with differential and CRP or ESR**, plus anti-TPO and IgE if available.`,`Do **not** order food allergy panels or elimination diets in CSU.`,`Wheals over 24 hours: **biopsy**, complement, ANA and urinalysis.`,`Angioedema without wheals: **C4 is the screening test** for HAE.`,`After anaphylaxis, **serum tryptase** at 1-2 hours confirms mast cell activation.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Step 1: a **second-generation H1 antihistamine daily**, not on demand.`,`Options: cetirizine 10 mg, levocetirizine 5 mg, fexofenadine 180 mg, bilastine 20 mg.`,`Step 2: after 2-4 weeks, **up-dose the same drug to 4 times** the standard dose.`,`First-generation sedating antihistamines are **not recommended**.`,`Avoid NSAIDs and use **paracetamol**; stop ACE inhibitors after angioedema.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Step 3: **omalizumab 300 mg SC every 4 weeks**, assess by 6 months.`,`**Dupilumab** and oral **remibrutinib 25 mg twice daily** are now step 3 alternatives.`,`Step 4: **ciclosporin 3.5-5 mg/kg/day** with BP and creatinine monitoring.`,`**Prednisolone** only as 3-7 day rescue; never long-term.`,`HAE attack: **icatibant 30 mg SC**, C1-INH concentrate or fresh frozen plasma.`,`In HAE, **adrenaline, antihistamines and steroids do not work**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Anaphylaxis: **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM** (1 mg/mL) into the anterolateral thigh.`,`Children: 0.3 mg at 6-12 years, 0.15 mg at 6 months-6 years; repeat after 5 minutes.`,`Lay the patient flat with legs raised; **never stand or walk** them.`,`Observe at least 6 hours; 12 hours or admit if more than one dose.`,`Tongue or laryngeal ACE-inhibitor or HAE angioedema - **secure the airway early**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review CSU **every 2-4 weeks** using UAS7; aim for complete control.`,`After 3 months of control, **step down** one tablet every 2-4 weeks.`,`Refer when 4-fold antihistamine fails, or for vasculitis or suspected HAE.`,`Refer everyone after anaphylaxis; screen first-degree relatives in HAE.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for urticaria and angioedema`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Levocetirizine or cetirizine`,`5 mg or 10 mg daily; up to 4 times daily`,`Regularly until controlled`,`Mild drowsiness; reduce in renal impairment`],[`Fexofenadine`,`180 mg daily; up to 2-3 times daily`,`Regularly`,`Not with fruit juice or antacids`],[`Bilastine`,`20 mg daily on empty stomach`,`Regularly`,`Headache; least sedating`],[`Omalizumab`,`300 mg SC every 4 weeks; age 12 and above`,`Assess by 6 months`,`Injection-site reaction; rare anaphylaxis`],[`Remibrutinib`,`25 mg twice daily orally (adults)`,`Add-on after antihistamine failure`,`Stop 3-7 days before surgery (bleeding)`],[`Ciclosporin`,`3.5-5 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`Limited months`,`Hypertension, nephrotoxicity; BP and creatinine`],[`Prednisolone`,`0.5 mg/kg (20-50 mg) daily`,`3-7 days rescue only`,`Rebound; never long term`],[`Adrenaline 1 mg/mL IM`,`0.5 mg adult, anterolateral thigh`,`Repeat after 5 minutes`,`Tachycardia, tremor; no absolute contraindication`],[`Icatibant`,`30 mg SC for HAE attack`,`Repeat after 6 h if needed`,`Injection-site pain; bradykinin angioedema only`]]},{heading:`Types of urticaria and angioedema`,columns:[`Type`,`Trigger or mechanism`,`Key clue`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Acute urticaria`,`Virus, drug, food, sting`,`Under 6 weeks`,`Remove trigger; daily antihistamine`],[`Chronic spontaneous`,`Autoimmune mast cell activation`,`Over 6 weeks, no trigger`,`Antihistamine ladder, omalizumab, ciclosporin`],[`Chronic inducible`,`Friction, cold, heat, pressure, sweat`,`Reproduced by provocation`,`Avoid trigger plus antihistamine`],[`Urticarial vasculitis`,`Immune complex venulitis`,`Over 24 h, burns, purpura`,`Dapsone, colchicine, steroids`],[`Bradykinin angioedema`,`ACE inhibitor or C1-INH deficiency`,`No wheals, no itch; low C4`,`Stop ACEI; icatibant, C1-INH, FFP`]]},{heading:`UAS7 activity bands`,columns:[`UAS7 score`,`Activity`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`0`,`Urticaria-free`,`Target of treatment`],[`1-6`,`Well controlled`,`Consider stepping down`],[`7-15`,`Mild`,`Optimise antihistamine`],[`16-27`,`Moderate`,`On 4-fold dose: omalizumab`],[`28-42`,`Severe`,`Step up promptly`]]}],remember:[`Ask how long one wheal lasts - over 24 hours is vasculitis until biopsied.`,`Urticaria is rubbed, not scratched.`,`Up-dose one antihistamine to 4 times before adding anything else.`,`Ladder: antihistamine, 4-fold, omalizumab, ciclosporin.`,`Angioedema without wheals is bradykinin - adrenaline and steroids fail.`,`Low C4 is the screening test for hereditary angioedema.`,`Anaphylaxis: IM adrenaline in the thigh first; everything else is adjunct.`]},updated:[`International Urticaria Guideline 2026 (GA2LEN/EuroGuiDerm/APAAACI, Allergy 2026): dupilumab and remibrutinib added as add-on options alongside omalizumab after failure of 4-fold antihistamine (conditional); never exceed 4-fold up-dosing`,`International Urticaria Guideline 2026: ciclosporin positioned for when licensed add-ons (omalizumab, dupilumab, remibrutinib) fail or are unavailable`,`Remibrutinib 25 mg twice daily (oral BTK inhibitor) FDA-approved for antihistamine-refractory CSU in adults, September 2025; dupilumab approved for CSU April 2025`,`Sebetralstat (oral plasma kallikrein inhibitor) FDA-approved July 2025 as first oral on-demand treatment of HAE attacks, age 12 and above`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Urticaria and angioedema`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Wheal** = transient itchy oedema with flare, **each lasting under 24 h, leaving no mark**; **angioedema** = deep dermal/subcutaneous swelling, painful or tingling, **up to 72 h**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Acute (6 weeks or less) vs chronic (over 6 weeks)**; chronic = **spontaneous (CSU)** or **inducible** (dermographism commonest, cold, pressure, cholinergic, solar, aquagenic); angioedema **histaminergic vs bradykinin** (ACE inhibitor, HAE, acquired C1-INH).`],[`Diagnostic criteria - activity scores`,`**UAS7 (0-42)**: 0 free, 1-6 well controlled, 7-15 mild, 16-27 moderate, 28-42 severe; **UCT 12 or more = controlled**.`],[`Aetiology`,`Acute: viral infection (children), **NSAIDs, beta-lactams**, foods, stings; CSU: **autoimmune type I (IgE anti-TPO, IL-24)** and **type IIb (IgG anti-FcepsilonRI)**; opioids, contrast act on MRGPRX2; NSAIDs aggravate a third.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Mast cell degranulation -> **histamine, tryptase, LTC4, PGD2, PAF** -> H1 on venules -> superficial dermal oedema (wheal) + axon reflex flare; deep -> angioedema; **ACE inhibitor/C1-INH loss -> bradykinin -> B2 receptor, no itch, no wheal**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**How long one wheal lasts** (pen circle), illness duration, burning/bruising (vasculitis), airway symptoms, triggers in prior 2 h, **drugs - NSAIDs, codeine, ACE inhibitor, oestrogen**, physical triggers, family history of swelling, systemic features, sleep impact, regular vs on-demand antihistamine use.`],[`Key examination`,`Evanescent wheals, normal skin between, **rubbed not scratched**; dermographism stroke test; airway, SpO2, BP; residual purpura (vasculitis); thyroid; nodes, spleen; **ice-cube test** for cold urticaria.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Acute: none routinely; CSU: **CBC with differential, CRP/ESR** (anti-TPO, total IgE if available); TSH, stool, H. pylori if suggested; **no allergy panels**; wheals over 24 h - biopsy, C3, C4; angioedema without wheals - **C4 (best HAE screen), C1-INH level and function**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Second-generation H1 antihistamine daily, regularly**: cetirizine 10 mg, levocetirizine 5 mg, fexofenadine 180 mg, bilastine 20 mg (empty stomach); **up-dose same drug to 4x after 2-4 weeks - never beyond 4x**; avoid first-generation sedating drugs.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`**Add omalizumab 300 mg SC every 4 weeks** (12 years and over; up-dose 450-600 mg or 2-weekly) **or dupilumab 300 mg every 2 weeks or remibrutinib 25 mg twice daily** (2026 update, conditional); then **ciclosporin 3.5-5 mg/kg/day** if licensed add-ons fail or unavailable; montelukast weak add-on.`],[`Treatment - rescue steroids`,`**Prednisolone 20-50 mg (0.5 mg/kg) for 3-7 days as rescue only**; **long-term systemic steroids have no place**.`],[`Emergency - anaphylaxis`,`**Adrenaline 1 mg/mL IM anterolateral thigh: 0.5 mg adult/over 12, 0.3 mg 6-12 y, 0.15 mg 6 months-6 y**, repeat after 5 min; lie flat; O2; fluids 500-1000 mL (10 mL/kg child); antihistamine after stabilisation; **hydrocortisone not routine**; observe 6-12 h.`],[`Treatment - HAE and ACE-inhibitor angioedema`,`Secure airway early; **icatibant 30 mg SC**, **C1-INH 20 IU/kg IV**, FFP 2 units if unavailable; **oral sebetralstat** on-demand (FDA 2025, 12+); prophylaxis - lanadelumab, berotralstat, tranexamic acid, danazol; **stop ACE inhibitor for life**; avoid oestrogen.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy/lactation**: loratadine or cetirizine, omalizumab if needed; children: cetirizine 2.5 mg twice daily (2-6 y); renal impairment: halve cetirizine/levocetirizine; elderly: avoid sedating antihistamines.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Tongue/throat swelling, stridor, hypotension (anaphylaxis), wheals over 24 h or bruising (vasculitis), systemic features, **angioedema without wheals** or low C4 (HAE, family screening), new angioedema after 40 (acquired C1-INH - lymphoma), 4-fold antihistamine failure.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review 2-4 weeks while stepping up with **UAS7/UCT**; once controlled 3 months, step down one tablet every 2-4 weeks; ciclosporin BP and creatinine 2-4 weekly; written anaphylaxis plan and auto-injector.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**International Urticaria Guideline 2026**: dupilumab and remibrutinib join omalizumab as add-ons; no up-dosing beyond 4x; ciclosporin when licensed options fail; **FDA 2025**: remibrutinib (CSU), sebetralstat (HAE attacks).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Ask how long one wheal lasts; urticaria is rubbed not scratched; up-dose one antihistamine to 4x before adding; angioedema without wheals is bradykinin - antihistamine, steroid, adrenaline fail; low C4 screens HAE; NSAIDs aggravate - use paracetamol.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`CSU ladder - International Urticaria Guideline 2026`,steps:[{label:`Standard-dose 2nd-gen antihistamine daily`,detail:`Levocetirizine 5 mg, fexofenadine 180 mg, bilastine 20 mg; review 2-4 weeks`},{label:`Up-dose same drug up to 4x`,detail:`Never beyond 4x; review 2-4 weeks with UAS7/UCT`},{label:`Add omalizumab, dupilumab or remibrutinib`,detail:`Omalizumab 300 mg every 4 weeks; dupilumab 300 mg 2-weekly; remibrutinib 25 mg BD`},{label:`Ciclosporin if add-ons fail or unavailable`,detail:`3.5-5 mg/kg/day; BP and creatinine every 2-4 weeks`},{label:`Rescue steroid only`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg 3-7 days; never long term`},{label:`Controlled 3 months - step down`,tone:`good`,detail:`Reduce one tablet every 2-4 weeks`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Swelling without wheals - bradykinin or histamine?`,steps:[{label:`Airway threatened?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Early intubation/surgical airway; IM adrenaline 0.5 mg may be tried`},{label:`Wheals, itch, allergen or drug trigger?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Yes = histaminergic: adrenaline, antihistamine, short steroid`},{label:`On ACE inhibitor?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Stop for life; icatibant or FFP if severe`},{label:`Recurrent, family history, abdominal attacks?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Check C4 then C1-INH level and function`},{label:`HAE attack treatment`,detail:`Icatibant 30 mg SC, C1-INH 20 IU/kg IV, oral sebetralstat, FFP`},{label:`Refer immunology; screen relatives`,tone:`good`,detail:`Plan prophylaxis: lanadelumab, berotralstat, tranexamic acid`}]}]},"dermatology-psoriasis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Psoriasis is a chronic **IL-23 and Th17-driven** disease of skin, nails and joints.`,`It affects about **0.5-1.5% of Indians**, with onset before or after 40 years.`,`**Psoriatic arthritis** affects 10-30% and is diagnosed by the **CASPAR criteria**.`,`It is a **systemic disease** linked to metabolic syndrome, fatty liver, heart disease and depression.`]},{title:`Classification and triggers`,points:[`**Chronic plaque** psoriasis is about 90%; others are guttate, inverse, scalp, palmoplantar, nail, erythrodermic and pustular.`,`**Rule of tens**: BSA, PASI or DLQI over 10 means moderate-to-severe disease.`,`Face, scalp, palms, soles, genitals or nails **upgrade mild disease**.`,`Erythrodermic and generalised pustular psoriasis are **emergencies**, often after **systemic steroid withdrawal**.`,`**Guttate psoriasis** follows streptococcal sore throat by 2-3 weeks.`,`Drugs: **beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials**, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, terbinafine.`,`HIV, smoking, alcohol, obesity, stress and trauma (Koebner) also trigger disease.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Genetic susceptibility, chiefly **HLA-C*06:02**, lowers the threshold for inflammation.`,`Injured keratinocytes release **LL-37**, which activates dendritic cells.`,`Dendritic cells secrete **IL-23**, which expands Th17 cells.`,`Th17 cells release **IL-17 and TNF-alpha**, driving keratinocyte proliferation in a loop.`,`Turnover falls from 28 days to **3-5 days**, giving silvery parakeratotic scale.`,`Dilated capillaries under thin epidermis explain the **Auspitz sign**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask age of onset, family history and recent sore throat.`,`Ask about **oral or injected steroids**, including from quacks.`,`Screen for arthritis: morning stiffness, heel pain, **dactylitis**, back pain.`,`Record **DLQI**; a score over 10 alone justifies systemic therapy.`,`Before systemic drugs ask about TB, hepatitis, HIV, liver and kidney disease.`,`Ask pregnancy plans in both sexes - methotrexate and acitretin are teratogenic.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Well-defined plaques with **silvery micaceous scale** on extensors, scalp and sacrum.`,`Show **grattage, Berkeley membrane and Auspitz sign** in order on one plaque.`,`Look for **Koebner phenomenon** - plaques along scratches or scars.`,`Examine scalp, ears, umbilicus, natal cleft, genitalia and nails.`,`Nails: **pits, oil-drop sign**, onycholysis and subungual hyperkeratosis.`,`Record BSA, PASI, weight, waist and blood pressure.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Psoriasis is a **clinical diagnosis**; biopsy only if atypical.`,`KOH for possible tinea; **VDRL** if palms and soles are involved.`,`Screen glucose, lipids, LFT and BP for comorbidity.`,`Before methotrexate: CBC, LFT, creatinine, hepatitis B and C, HIV, pregnancy test.`,`Before biologics: **TB screening** with IGRA or tuberculin test plus chest X-ray.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Remove scale first with **salicylic acid 3-6%**, then apply the active drug.`,`**Calcipotriol plus betamethasone** once daily for 4 weeks, then twice weekly.`,`Calcipotriol maximum **100 g per week**; salicylic acid inactivates it.`,`Potent steroid on body; mild steroid or tacrolimus on face and flexures.`,`Coal tar and short-contact **dithranol** are cheap alternatives.`,`**Never use systemic corticosteroids** for psoriasis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**NB-UVB (311 nm)** 2-3 times weekly is first line for extensive disease.`,`**Methotrexate 7.5-25 mg once weekly** with folic acid 5 mg on another day.`,`Methotrexate is the choice when **psoriatic arthritis** coexists.`,`**Ciclosporin 2.5-5 mg/kg/day** gives rapid control; limit to 1 year.`,`**Acitretin** suits pustular, palmoplantar and HIV patients - it is not immunosuppressive.`,`**Apremilast 30 mg twice daily**, then biologics after failed standard systemic therapy.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: emollients, topical steroids, NB-UVB, ciclosporin or certolizumab.`,`Never methotrexate or acitretin in pregnancy.`,`Acitretin needs **contraception for 3 years** after stopping and no alcohol.`,`**Children**: topicals, NB-UVB, methotrexate, etanercept or adalimumab.`,`**HIV**: antiretroviral therapy, phototherapy and acitretin first.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Erythroderma** over 90% BSA - admit for warmth, fluids and electrolytes.`,`**Generalised pustular psoriasis** (fever, pus lakes, low calcium and albumin) or **impetigo herpetiformis** in pregnancy - admit urgently.`,`Mouth ulcers or fever on methotrexate - stop it and give **folinic acid**.`,`New cough or fever on a biologic means **TB reactivation** until excluded.`,`Review topicals at **4 weeks**; aim for **PASI 75** and DLQI 0-1 on systemic therapy.`,`Methotrexate: CBC, LFT, creatinine every 1-2 weeks for a month, then 2-3 monthly; ciclosporin: BP, creatinine 2-weekly for 3 months.`,`Check cardiovascular risk yearly; refer suspected arthritis to rheumatology.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for psoriasis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Calcipotriol plus betamethasone`,`Topical once daily; calcipotriol max 100 g/week`,`4 weeks, then twice weekly`,`Irritation; not with salicylic acid`],[`Clobetasol propionate 0.05%`,`Topical once daily, palms, soles, scalp`,`2-4 weeks, then taper`,`Atrophy; rebound if stopped abruptly`],[`Dithranol`,`Short contact 0.1-3% for 10-30 min`,`Daily, increasing strength`,`Burning, purple-brown staining`],[`NB-UVB (311 nm)`,`2-3 sessions weekly`,`About 20-30 sessions`,`Erythema, burning; safe in pregnancy`],[`Methotrexate`,`7.5-25 mg once weekly plus folic acid 5 mg`,`Response in 8-12 weeks`,`Cytopenia, liver fibrosis; never daily`],[`Ciclosporin`,`2.5-5 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`Maximum 1 year`,`Hypertension, nephrotoxicity`],[`Acitretin`,`25-50 mg daily with fatty meal`,`Response in 2-3 months`,`Teratogen for 3 years; raised lipids`],[`Apremilast`,`Titrate to 30 mg twice daily`,`Long term`,`Diarrhoea, weight loss, depression`],[`Adalimumab`,`80 mg SC, then 40 mg every 2 weeks`,`Assess at 12-16 weeks`,`TB reactivation, heart failure`],[`Secukinumab`,`300 mg SC weekly for 5 weeks, then monthly`,`Assess at 12-16 weeks`,`Candidiasis, IBD flare`]]},{heading:`Rule of tens - severity and treatment`,columns:[`Severity`,`Criteria`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Mild`,`BSA, PASI and DLQI all 10 or less`,`Topicals and emollients`],[`Moderate to severe`,`Any one over 10`,`NB-UVB or standard systemic drugs`],[`Upgraded`,`Special site or topical failure`,`Treat as moderate to severe`],[`Biologic candidate`,`PASI 10+, DLQI over 10, failed systemic`,`Anti-TNF, IL-17, IL-23, IL-12/23`],[`Emergency`,`Erythroderma or generalised pustular`,`Admit; ciclosporin, infliximab, acitretin`]]},{heading:`Types of psoriasis`,columns:[`Type`,`Key features`,`Preferred treatment`],rows:[[`Chronic plaque`,`Silvery plaques on extensors, scalp, sacrum`,`Topicals; UVB or systemic if extensive`],[`Guttate`,`Drop-like trunk papules after strep throat`,`Treat strep, topicals, NB-UVB`],[`Inverse`,`Shiny red plaques without scale in folds`,`Mild steroid, tacrolimus`],[`Palmoplantar pustulosis`,`Sterile pustules on palms and soles`,`Stop smoking; acitretin, PUVA`],[`Generalised pustular`,`Fever, sterile pustule sheets, low calcium`,`Admit; acitretin, ciclosporin, spesolimab`],[`Erythrodermic`,`Over 90% BSA, systemically unwell`,`Admit; ciclosporin or infliximab`]]}],remember:[`Never give systemic steroids in psoriasis - withdrawal flare can kill.`,`Grattage, Berkeley membrane, Auspitz sign - in that order.`,`Rule of tens: BSA, PASI or DLQI over 10 means systemic therapy.`,`Methotrexate once weekly on a fixed day; daily dosing kills.`,`Salicylic acid inactivates calcipotriol.`,`Acitretin: not immunosuppressive, but 3 years of contraception.`,`Screen TB, hepatitis and HIV before any biologic.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Psoriasis`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Chronic relapsing **IL-23/Th17-driven** inflammatory disease of skin, nails and joints; **0.5-1.5% of Indians**; bimodal onset (type I before 40, HLA-Cw6).`],[`Relevant classification`,`Chronic plaque (90%), guttate, inverse, scalp, palmoplantar, nail, **erythrodermic, pustular (von Zumbusch)**; severity **rule of tens - BSA, PASI, DLQI over 10 = moderate-severe**; special sites (face, scalp, palms, soles, genitals, nails) upgrade.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`PSORS1/HLA-C*06:02, IL36RN (pustular); triggers - **streptococcal throat (guttate)**, trauma (Koebner), **beta-blockers, lithium, antimalarials, NSAIDs, steroid withdrawal**, HIV, smoking, alcohol, obesity, stress.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Injury -> LL-37 + self-DNA -> pDC IFN-alpha -> mDC **IL-23** -> **Th17: IL-17A/F, IL-22, TNF** -> keratinocyte proliferation (turnover **3-5 days**) -> parakeratosis, acanthosis, Munro microabscesses -> silvery plaque; systemic inflammation -> metabolic/CV disease.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Onset, family history, sore throat, drugs (**oral/injected steroids from quacks**), **joint pain, morning stiffness, heel pain, dactylitis** (PEST), DLQI, prior treatments, comorbidities, TB/HBV/HCV/HIV before systemics, pregnancy plans.`],[`Key examination`,`Well-defined plaques with **silvery micaceous scale** on extensors, scalp, sacrum; **grattage -> Berkeley membrane -> Auspitz**; Koebner; hidden sites (scalp, ears, umbilicus, natal cleft, genitals); **nails - pits, oil drop, onycholysis**; joints/entheses; BSA, PASI, BMI, BP.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Clinical; biopsy if atypical; KOH, VDRL (palms/soles); ASO in guttate; **HbA1c, lipids, LFT, BP**; before methotrexate CBC, LFT, creatinine, HBV, HCV, HIV, pregnancy test, PIIINP/FibroScan; before biologics **TB screen (IGRA/TST + CXR)**.`],[`Treatment - first line (mild)`,`Keratolytic **salicylic acid 3-6%** (separate from calcipotriol) -> **calcipotriol + betamethasone once daily up to 4 weeks**, then twice weekly; calcipotriol max **100 g/week**; potent steroid trunk; tacrolimus face/flexures; coal tar; dithranol short contact.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`**NB-UVB 2-3 times weekly**; **methotrexate 7.5-25 mg ONCE WEEKLY + folic acid 5 mg weekly** (PsA); **ciclosporin 2.5-5 mg/kg/day max 1 year** (rapid control); **acitretin 25-50 mg** (pustular, HIV); **apremilast 30 mg BD**; deucravacitinib 6 mg.`],[`Treatment - biologics`,`PASI 10 or more and DLQI over 10 after failed systemic: **anti-TNF** (adalimumab 80 mg then 40 mg 2-weekly), **anti-IL-17** (secukinumab 300 mg), **anti-IL-23** (guselkumab, risankizumab), ustekinumab; itolizumab, biosimilars; screen TB, HBV.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: emollient, topical steroid, NB-UVB, ciclosporin or certolizumab - **never methotrexate or acitretin** (acitretin contraception 3 years); children: topicals, NB-UVB, methotrexate, etanercept/adalimumab; HIV: ART, phototherapy, acitretin.`],[`Complications`,`Psoriatic arthritis (10-30%, CASPAR), metabolic syndrome, CVD, NAFLD, depression, IBD; **erythroderma** (heat and fluid loss, sepsis, high-output failure); **GPP** (hypocalcaemia, hypoalbuminaemia); drug toxicity.`],[`Emergencies`,`Erythroderma: warm room, fluids, protein, ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg or infliximab 5 mg/kg; **GPP: acitretin 0.5-1 mg/kg, ciclosporin, infliximab or spesolimab 900 mg IV**; impetigo herpetiformis - admit, ciclosporin or steroid under specialist.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Erythroderma, fever with sterile pustules, **flare after steroid course**, inflammatory back pain/dactylitis (rheumatology), mouth ulcers/bruising on methotrexate (stop, folinic acid), cough/fever on biologic (TB), non-responding plaque (biopsy).`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Topicals 4 weeks; methotrexate CBC/LFT/creatinine **1-2 weekly for a month then 2-3 monthly** (stop WBC under 3500, platelets under 100,000, ALT over 3x); ciclosporin BP/creatinine 2-weekly 3 months; biologic response at 12-16 weeks; yearly CV risk.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Never systemic steroids** - pustular/erythrodermic withdrawal flare; methotrexate once weekly, never daily; salicylic acid inactivates calcipotriol; acitretin not immunosuppressive but 3 years contraception; screen TB before biologics.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Psoriasis escalation by severity`,steps:[{label:`Mild: emollient + keratolytic`,detail:`Salicylic acid 3-6% to lift scale, emollient twice daily`},{label:`Calcipotriol-betamethasone once daily`,detail:`Up to 4 weeks, then twice weekly; calcipotriol max 100 g/week`},{label:`NB-UVB 2-3 times weekly`,detail:`Extensive plaque or guttate; safe in pregnancy and children`},{label:`Conventional systemic`,detail:`Methotrexate 7.5-25 mg weekly + folic acid; ciclosporin; acitretin; apremilast`},{label:`Biologic after TB and hepatitis screen`,tone:`good`,detail:`Anti-TNF, anti-IL-17, anti-IL-23, anti-IL-12/23`},{label:`Never systemic steroid`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Withdrawal precipitates pustular or erythrodermic flare`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Choosing the systemic by patient profile`,columns:[`Situation`,`Prefer`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Psoriatic arthritis`,`Methotrexate; anti-TNF or anti-IL-17`,`Acitretin alone`],[`Rapid control, erythroderma`,`Ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg; infliximab`,`Slow agents alone`],[`Pustular or palmoplantar`,`Acitretin; spesolimab for GPP`,`Systemic steroid`],[`HIV or cancer history`,`Acitretin, phototherapy, ART`,`Immunosuppressants`],[`Pregnancy`,`NB-UVB, ciclosporin, certolizumab`,`Methotrexate, acitretin`],[`Inflammatory bowel disease`,`Anti-TNF, anti-IL-23`,`Anti-IL-17`]]}]},"dermatology-leprosy-nlep":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Leprosy is chronic infection of **skin and peripheral nerves** by Mycobacterium leprae.`,`Disability comes from **nerve damage**; the disease is **curable with MDT**.`,`Diagnosis needs **any one cardinal sign** of the three.`,`Cardinal signs: **anaesthetic patch**, thickened nerve with deficit, positive slit-skin smear.`,`India reports **over half the world's new cases**, about 1 lakh a year.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**PB**: 1-5 skin lesions, no or one nerve trunk, smear negative.`,`**MB**: 6 or more lesions, more than one nerve trunk, or smear positive.`,`**A positive smear always means MB.**`,`Ridley-Jopling spectrum: TT, BT, BB, BL, LL; borderline forms are unstable and react.`,`WHO disability: **Grade 0** none, **Grade 1** sensory loss, **Grade 2** visible deformity.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`M. leprae cannot be cultured and doubles every **12-14 days**, so incubation is years.`,`Spread is by **nasal droplets** from untreated MB patients; over 95% of exposed people are immune.`,`It prefers **cool tissues** - skin, superficial nerves, nose, earlobes, testes.`,`It invades **Schwann cells**, so sensory and autonomic loss come before motor loss.`,`Strong Th1 immunity gives **tuberculoid** disease; anergy gives **lepromatous** disease.`,`Nerves thicken where superficial or in tunnels - ulnar at elbow, common peroneal at fibular neck.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask whether the patch is **numb, dry or does not sweat**.`,`Ask about tingling, weakness, painless burns and foot drop.`,`New nerve pain or swelling of old patches suggests a **reaction**.`,`Fever with crops of painful nodules suggests **ENL**.`,`Ask about contacts, previous MDT, G6PD deficiency, pregnancy and stigma.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine the whole body in daylight and **count the lesions**.`,`Test sensation with **eyes closed** using cotton, pin and warm and cold tubes.`,`Temperature is lost first, then touch, then pain.`,`Use a **10 g monofilament** on palms and soles for protective sensation.`,`Palpate nerves bilaterally: great auricular, ulnar, radial cutaneous, common peroneal, posterior tibial.`,`Do **voluntary muscle testing**: eye closure, little finger, thumb, wrist, ankle dorsiflexion.`,`Record the **disability grade** for each eye, hand and foot.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Leprosy is a **clinical diagnosis** - start MDT without waiting for tests.`,`**Slit-skin smear** from both earlobes, forehead and the most active lesion edge.`,`Report the **bacteriological index** on a log scale of 0 to 6+.`,`Check haemoglobin and, where available, **G6PD** before dapsone.`,`Before long steroids: glucose, BP, TB screen and **albendazole** for Strongyloides.`,`The lepromin test is **not diagnostic**; it only classifies.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`India gives **three-drug MDT** to both PB and MB patients.`,`Adult: monthly supervised **rifampicin 600 mg plus clofazimine 300 mg**; daily **clofazimine 50 mg plus dapsone 100 mg**.`,`**PB 6 months** (6 packs within 9 months); **MB 12 months** (12 packs within 18 months).`,`Rifampicin on an empty stomach; clofazimine with food or milk.`,`MDT is safe in **pregnancy and breastfeeding**; **never stop MDT** for a reaction, pregnancy or lactation.`,`Child 10-14 years: rifampicin 450 mg and clofazimine 150 mg monthly, dapsone 50 mg daily; under 10, **dose by weight**, never an adult pack.`,`With TB, give full anti-TB treatment plus clofazimine and dapsone.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Mild reaction: continue MDT with rest and analgesics.`,`Type 1 reaction or neuritis: **prednisolone 40 mg daily tapered over 12 weeks**.`,`Splint and rest the affected limb during neuritis.`,`Severe ENL: **prednisolone 40-60 mg** tapered over 12-20 weeks.`,`Recurrent ENL: add **clofazimine 100 mg three times daily** up to 3 months.`,`**Thalidomide** for severe ENL at referral level only; never in women who could conceive.`,`Rifampicin weakens oral contraceptives and steroids - increase the prednisolone dose.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`New weakness or nerve pain is **acute neuritis** - start prednisolone the same day.`,`Inability to close the eye or a red painful eye needs urgent eye referral.`,`Fever, rash and jaundice within 6 weeks means **dapsone hypersensitivity** - stop it for life.`,`Plantar ulcers need offloading; probing to bone means osteomyelitis.`,`See monthly for the supervised dose; **VMT and sensory testing** every visit catch **silent neuritis**.`,`After release, review for **2 years (PB)** and **5 years (MB)**.`,`**Relapse** is slow and painless; a **reaction** is sudden, painful and steroid-responsive.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Patients are **non-infectious within days** of rifampicin - no isolation needed.`,`Contacts aged **2 years and above** get single-dose rifampicin (SDR-PEP).`,`Self-care for anaesthetic limbs: **soak, scrape, oil and inspect** daily.`,`NLEP aims to **interrupt transmission by 2027** (National Strategic Plan 2023-2027).`,`Programme tools: ABSULS, Leprosy Case Detection Campaigns, Sparsh campaign, Nikusth 2.0.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for leprosy and reactions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Rifampicin`,`600 mg monthly, supervised, oral`,`6 (PB) or 12 (MB) months`,`Orange urine, hepatitis; enzyme inducer`],[`Clofazimine`,`300 mg monthly plus 50 mg daily`,`6 or 12 months`,`Red-brown pigmentation, dry skin`],[`Dapsone`,`100 mg daily, oral`,`6 or 12 months`,`Haemolysis in G6PD deficiency; hypersensitivity`],[`Prednisolone (type 1, neuritis)`,`40 mg daily, morning, tapered`,`12 weeks`,`Hyperglycaemia, TB reactivation; give albendazole first`],[`Prednisolone (ENL)`,`40-60 mg daily, tapered`,`12-20 weeks or longer`,`As above; never stop suddenly`],[`Clofazimine (ENL)`,`100 mg three times daily`,`Up to 3 months, then taper`,`Crystal enteropathy at high dose`],[`Thalidomide (ENL)`,`100-400 mg nightly, referral level`,`Taper to 50-100 mg`,`Phocomelia; never if pregnancy possible`],[`Rifampicin SDR-PEP`,`Single dose 600 mg adult`,`Once`,`Exclude leprosy, TB, pregnancy first`]]},{heading:`NLEP classification and MDT`,columns:[`Group`,`Criteria`,`Adult MDT`,`Completion`],rows:[[`Paucibacillary`,`1-5 lesions, up to 1 nerve, smear negative`,`Three-drug MDT`,`6 packs within 9 months`],[`Multibacillary`,`6+ lesions, 2+ nerves, or smear positive`,`Three-drug MDT`,`12 packs within 18 months`],[`Pure neuritic`,`Nerve involvement, no skin lesion`,`PB if one nerve, MB if more`,`As PB or MB`]]},{heading:`Lepra reactions compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 1 (reversal)`,`Type 2 (ENL)`],rows:[[`Mechanism`,`Type IV, rising cell immunity`,`Type III immune complex, high TNF`],[`Patients`,`Borderline: BT, BB, BL`,`BL and LL`],[`Skin`,`Old patches red and swollen`,`Crops of tender new nodules`],[`Systemic`,`Little fever; acute neuritis`,`Fever, iritis, orchitis, arthritis`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone 40 mg over 12 weeks`,`Prednisolone, clofazimine; thalidomide at referral`]]}],remember:[`One cardinal sign is enough for diagnosis.`,`A positive smear makes it MB regardless of lesion count.`,`Leprosy patch: hypopigmented, dry, hairless, anaesthetic; vitiligo is chalk-white with normal sensation.`,`Temperature goes first, then touch, then pain.`,`Three-drug MDT for all: PB 6 months, MB 12 months.`,`Never stop MDT for a reaction, pregnancy or lactation.`,`Self-care mnemonic: soak, scrape, oil, inspect.`,`Clofazimine's red-brown skin pigmentation is the commonest cause of default.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Leprosy under NLEP`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Chronic granulomatous infection of **skin and peripheral nerves** by **M. leprae**; disability from nerve damage; **curable with free fixed-duration MDT**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria (NLEP)`,`**Any ONE cardinal sign**: (1) hypopigmented/erythematous patch with **definite sensory loss**, (2) **thickened nerve with sensory loss or weakness**, (3) **AFB on slit-skin smear**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**PB: 1-5 lesions, 0-1 nerve, smear negative; MB: 6 or more lesions, more than one nerve, or any positive smear**; Ridley-Jopling TT-BT-BB-BL-LL (borderline unstable - reactions); **WHO disability grade 0/1/2** per eye, hand, foot; EHF score 0-12.`],[`Epidemiology and programme`,`India has over half of world new cases (about 1 lakh/year); elimination (under 1/10,000) 2005; **NSP and Roadmap 2023-2027 - zero transmission by 2027**; Sparsh campaign, LCDC, ABSULS, **Nikusth 2.0**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Droplet spread from untreated MB -> **doubling 12-14 days**, prefers 27-33 C -> **PGL-1 binds laminin-alpha2 on Schwann cells** -> sensory (temperature first) and autonomic loss -> motor loss -> anaesthesia -> trophic ulcers and deformity; Th1 (tuberculoid) vs Th2 (lepromatous).`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Numb, dry patch; tingling, weakness, dropping objects, painless burns, foot drop; nerve pain or reddening of old patches (reaction); fever with tender nodules (ENL); eye symptoms; contact history; previous MDT; G6PD/anaemia; stigma.`],[`Key examination`,`Count lesions; **sensation eyes closed** (cotton, pin, hot/cold tubes); **10 g monofilament** palms/soles; **palpate nerves bilaterally** (great auricular, ulnar, median, radial cutaneous, common peroneal, posterior tibial, sural); **VMT**; eyes (lagophthalmos); deformities; examine contacts.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Clinical diagnosis - start MDT without waiting; **slit-skin smear** (both earlobes, forehead, active lesion) - **BI 0-6+**, any positive = MB; Hb and G6PD; biopsy for doubtful/pure neuritic; pre-steroid glucose, BP, albendazole.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Uniform three-drug MDT for PB and MB (NLEP 2025)**: adult monthly supervised **rifampicin 600 mg + clofazimine 300 mg**, daily **clofazimine 50 mg + dapsone 100 mg**; **PB 6 packs in 9 months, MB 12 packs in 18 months**; child 10-14 y monthly rifampicin 450 + clofazimine 150, daily dapsone 50 + clofazimine 50 alternate days.`],[`Treatment - lepra reactions`,`Never stop MDT; **type 1/neuritis: prednisolone 40 mg (up to 1 mg/kg) tapered over 12 weeks** (20-24 weeks in MB, pure neuritic) + splint; **ENL: prednisolone 40-60 mg over 12-20 weeks**, recurrent - **clofazimine 100 mg TDS up to 3 months**; **thalidomide** at referral level, never in women who may conceive.`],[`Drug side effects`,`Rifampicin - orange secretions, hepatitis, enzyme induction (OCP failure, raise steroid dose); clofazimine - **red-black pigmentation**, ichthyosis, enteropathy; dapsone - **haemolysis (G6PD), methaemoglobinaemia, DHS (HLA-B*13:01)**.`],[`Special groups`,`MDT safe in **pregnancy and lactation**; TB co-infection - full ATT with daily rifampicin + clofazimine + dapsone; HIV - standard MDT, more reactions; child under 10 - weight-based doses.`],[`Prevention and contacts`,`Examine household contacts; **SDR-PEP rifampicin single dose** (600 mg adult, 450 mg 10-14 y, 300 mg 6-9 y, 10-15 mg/kg 2-5 y) after excluding leprosy, TB, pregnancy - about 50-60% risk reduction; **DPMR**: MCR footwear, splints, free RCS with welfare allowance.`],[`Prevention - self-care (DPMR)`,`**Soak, scrape, oil, inspect** anaesthetic hands and feet daily; never barefoot; offload plantar ulcers; lagophthalmos - blink exercises, tears, night cover.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`New weakness or nerve pain (steroid today), red painful eye, severe ENL, **DHS within 6 weeks**, haemolysis, infected ulcer/osteomyelitis, suspected relapse (rising BI years after RFT), drug resistance.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Monthly **VMT + ST** catches silent neuritis; RFT surveillance **2 years PB, 5 years MB**; relapse slow and painless vs reaction sudden and painful; indicators - NCDR, **G2D per million**, child proportion.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Uniform three-drug MDT for PB as well as MB (NLEP/MoHFW 2025)** - PB 6 months, MB 12 months; SDR remains the PEP regimen.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`One cardinal sign is enough; positive smear = MB; temperature lost first; never stop MDT for reaction or pregnancy; non-infectious within days of rifampicin - no isolation; clofazimine pigmentation is the commonest cause of default.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected leprosy - diagnose, classify, treat`,steps:[{label:`Any one cardinal sign present?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Anaesthetic patch, thick nerve with deficit, or AFB on smear`},{label:`Count lesions, nerves; smear if available`,tone:`decision`,detail:`PB 1-5, 0-1 nerve, smear negative; otherwise MB`},{label:`Start uniform 3-drug MDT today`,detail:`Rif 600 + Clo 300 monthly; Clo 50 + Dap 100 daily`},{label:`Grade disability, VMT and ST baseline`,detail:`WHO grade 0/1/2 for eyes, hands, feet; EHF score`},{label:`Reaction or neuritis at any visit?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Prednisolone 40 mg tapered 12 weeks; continue MDT`},{label:`Contacts examined, SDR-PEP given`,tone:`good`,detail:`Rifampicin 600 mg single dose age 15+, weight-based 2-14 y`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Type 1 reaction vs ENL vs relapse`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 1 (reversal)`,`Type 2 (ENL)`,`Relapse`],rows:[[`Who`,`BT, BB, BL`,`BL, LL`,`After RFT`],[`Mechanism`,`Type IV, rising CMI`,`Type III immune complex, TNF`,`Surviving bacilli`],[`Onset`,`Sudden, first 6 months of MDT`,`Sudden, recurrent`,`Slow, years after RFT`],[`Features`,`Old patches red, swollen; neuritis`,`Crops of tender nodules, fever, iritis, orchitis`,`New painless lesions, BI rises 2+`],[`Treatment`,`Prednisolone 40 mg over 12 weeks`,`Prednisolone 40-60 mg; clofazimine; thalidomide`,`Full MDT again`]]}]},"dermatology-drug-eruptions-sjs-ten":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition, key facts and culprits`,points:[`Drug rashes affect about **2-3% of hospitalised patients**; over 90% are benign exanthems.`,`**Type A** reactions are dose-dependent; **type B** are immunological, unpredictable, and include all the dangerous rashes.`,`**Withdrawing the culprit drug** is the single act that most changes mortality.`,`High-risk drugs: **carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine, allopurinol, cotrimoxazole**, dapsone, nevirapine, oxicam NSAIDs, anti-tubercular drugs, fluoroquinolones.`,`**HLA-B*15:02** with carbamazepine; **HLA-B*58:01** with allopurinol; HLA-B*57:01 with abacavir.`,`**HIV** raises SJS-TEN risk up to 100-fold.`,`Aromatic anticonvulsants **cross-react** with each other.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Benign: morbilliform, urticarial, fixed and photosensitive eruptions.`,`**Severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SCAR)**: SJS and TEN, DRESS, AGEP.`,`Detached plus detachable skin: **SJS under 10%**, overlap 10-30%, **TEN over 30%**.`,`**Erythema multiforme major** is separate - typical acral targets, usually after herpes simplex.`,`Onset: AGEP 1-4 days, exanthem 4-14 days, **SJS-TEN 4-28 days**, DRESS 2-8 weeks.`,`Mortality: AGEP under 5%, DRESS about 10%, SJS 5-10%, **TEN 30% or more**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Drugs act as **haptens** or bind HLA directly (p-i concept), so first exposure can react.`,`The 4-28 day latency is the time to prime drug-specific T cells.`,`In SJS-TEN, **CD8 T cells and NK cells** release **granulysin**, the main effector.`,`Mass keratinocyte apoptosis gives **full-thickness epidermal necrosis**, so skin slides off.`,`Loss of barrier causes fluid loss, heat loss and **sepsis**, the leading cause of death.`,`In DRESS, IL-5 drives eosinophilia and **HHV-6 reactivation** prolongs the course.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Make a **drug chart for the last 8 weeks** against the date of rash onset.`,`Include OTC, ayurvedic, homoeopathic and injected drugs.`,`**Skin pain** out of proportion to the rash is the earliest warning.`,`Same lesion at the same site after a tablet means **fixed drug eruption**.`,`Ask about eye pain, painful swallowing, painful urination and breathlessness.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record pulse, BP, temperature, SpO2 and urine output first.`,`SJS-TEN shows **dusky flat atypical targets** and flaccid bullae.`,`Elicit the **Nikolsky sign**; count only detached or Nikolsky-positive skin, never erythema.`,`Two or more eroded **mucosal sites** make a SCAR likely.`,`DRESS triad: **facial oedema, lymph nodes, liver**.`,`Examine the eyes on day 1 for pseudomembrane and early symblepharon.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC: eosinophilia over 700 supports DRESS; neutrophilia supports AGEP.`,`Glucose, urea, bicarbonate and electrolytes feed **SCORTEN**.`,`LFT, urine; in DRESS add troponin, ECG, TSH and lipase.`,`Swabs and blood cultures at baseline; chest X-ray and SpO2.`,`**Frozen section** separates TEN from staphylococcal scalded skin within hours.`,`**Never rechallenge** or skin-test after SJS-TEN or DRESS.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Stop the culprit and all non-essential drugs immediately.**`,`Assign causality with the **ALDEN algorithm** (Naranjo for other reactions).`,`Transfer to burns unit or ICU if **detachment over 10% or SCORTEN 2 or more**.`,`Nurse at **25-28 C**; leave detached epidermis in place as a dressing.`,`Use paraffin emollients; **avoid silver sulfadiazine** - it is a sulfonamide.`,`Fluids about **2 mL/kg per % BSA** over 24 hours; aim urine 0.5-1 mL/kg/hour.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day** for 7-10 days has the most consistent survival benefit.`,`**Etanercept 50 mg SC** single dose is an alternative.`,`Short early steroids only **within 72 hours**, for 3-5 days, then rapid taper.`,`IVIG alone has **not shown consistent benefit**.`,`DRESS with organ involvement: **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day** tapered over 6-12 weeks.`,`AGEP needs drug withdrawal and topical steroid - **no antibiotics**.`,`Paracetamol and opioids for pain; **avoid NSAIDs**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Stridor, hypoxia or haemoptysis means bronchial involvement - **intubate early**.`,`Daily ophthalmology from day 1 - **symblepharon** forms within days.`,`Sepsis from S. aureus and Pseudomonas - antibiotics only when proven.`,`Late sequelae: dry eye, strictures, nail loss, bronchiolitis obliterans.`,`DRESS can cause late **autoimmune thyroiditis** and diabetes.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer same day for skin pain, mucosal erosion, blisters, Nikolsky sign or facial oedema.`,`**Lifelong avoidance** of the culprit and its cross-reacting class.`,`Give a **drug alert card** and report to **PvPI** (helpline 1800-180-3024).`,`In DRESS check TSH and glucose at 3, 6 and 12 months.`,`After carbamazepine SJS use **levetiracetam or valproate**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in drug eruptions and SJS-TEN`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Cetirizine`,`10 mg once daily orally`,`1-2 weeks`,`Drowsiness; benign exanthem only`],[`Mometasone 0.1% cream`,`Once daily, topical`,`5-7 days`,`Not on eroded skin`],[`Adrenaline 1 mg/mL`,`0.5 mg IM thigh; child 0.01 mg/kg`,`Repeat every 5 minutes`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia`],[`Ciclosporin`,`3-5 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`7-10 days, then taper`,`Nephrotoxicity; daily creatinine and BP`],[`Etanercept`,`50 mg SC once (25 mg if under 65 kg)`,`Single dose`,`Infection`],[`Prednisolone (DRESS)`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day orally`,`Taper over 6-12 weeks or more`,`Relapse if tapered fast`],[`Carboxymethylcellulose 0.5% drops`,`Every 1-2 hours, preservative-free`,`Acute phase`,`Transient blur`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC once daily`,`While immobile`,`Bleeding; check platelets`]]},{heading:`Severe cutaneous adverse reactions compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`SJS-TEN`,`DRESS`,`AGEP`],rows:[[`Onset after drug`,`4-28 days`,`2-8 weeks`,`1-4 days`],[`Skin`,`Dusky targets, Nikolsky positive`,`Infiltrated rash, facial oedema`,`Pinhead sterile pustules`],[`Mucosa`,`Two or more sites eroded`,`Mild or spared`,`Usually spared`],[`Blood`,`Lymphopenia`,`Eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytes`,`Neutrophilia`],[`Organs`,`Eye, lung, kidney`,`Liver, kidney, heart`,`Rarely`],[`Mortality`,`SJS 5-10%, TEN 30%+`,`About 10%`,`Under 5%`]]},{heading:`SCORTEN (within 24 hours and on day 3)`,columns:[`Component`,`Finding`,`Points`],rows:[[`Age`,`40 years or more`,`1`],[`Malignancy`,`Present`,`1`],[`Heart rate`,`120 per minute or more`,`1`],[`Detachment on day 1`,`Over 10% body surface`,`1`],[`Serum urea`,`Over 10 mmol/L`,`1`],[`Serum bicarbonate`,`Under 20 mmol/L`,`1`],[`Serum glucose`,`Over 14 mmol/L (252 mg/dL)`,`1`]]}],remember:[`First treatment of SJS-TEN is stopping the culprit drug.`,`SJS under 10%, overlap 10-30%, TEN over 30% - count detached skin, not erythema.`,`Onset: AGEP 1-4 days, SJS-TEN 4-28 days, DRESS 2-8 weeks.`,`SCORTEN 2 or more means ICU; 5 or more predicts about 90% mortality.`,`No silver sulfadiazine - it is a sulfonamide.`,`Fixed drug eruption: same site, within hours, slate-grey pigment.`,`Never rechallenge after SJS-TEN or DRESS.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Drug eruptions and SJS/TEN`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Adverse cutaneous drug reaction at normal dose; 2-3% of inpatients; **over 90% benign exanthems**; dangerous ones are **type B immunological SCARs**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Benign: morbilliform, urticarial, **fixed**, photosensitive; **SCAR: SJS/TEN, DRESS, AGEP**; **SJS under 10%, overlap 10-30%, TEN over 30%** detached + detachable BSA; EM major separate (HSV, typical raised targets).`],[`Diagnostic clue - time to onset`,`Urticaria minutes-hours; FDE hours on re-exposure; **AGEP 1-4 days**; exanthem 4-14 days; **SJS/TEN 4-28 days**; **DRESS 2-8 weeks**.`],[`Aetiology`,`**Carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbitone, lamotrigine, allopurinol, cotrimoxazole/sulfonamides, dapsone, nevirapine, oxicam NSAIDs, ATT**; HIV raises risk up to 100-fold; Mycoplasma in children; **HLA-B*15:02 carbamazepine, B*58:01 allopurinol, B*57:01 abacavir**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Drug (hapten/p-i) -> CD8 T cells and NK -> **granulysin, perforin, Fas-FasL** -> keratinocyte apoptosis -> **full-thickness necrosis, subepidermal split** -> Nikolsky, mucosal erosions -> fluid loss, sepsis; DRESS IL-5 + HHV-6 reactivation; AGEP IL-8 neutrophils.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Drug chart of last 8 weeks** with dates incl. OTC/ayurvedic; **prodrome of fever and skin pain**; eye, mouth, genital, urinary symptoms; breathlessness; facial swelling, jaundice (DRESS); same-site recurrence (FDE); HIV, epilepsy, gout, TB.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals first; **dusky flat atypical targets, flaccid bullae, Nikolsky**; % detachment by rule of nines (not erythema); **eyes day 1**, lips, genitals (2+ mucosae = SCAR); facial oedema, nodes, liver (DRESS); pinhead pustules (AGEP).`],[`Minimal investigations`,`CBC (eosinophils over 700 in DRESS), **glucose, urea, bicarbonate** (SCORTEN), LFT, creatinine, urine, troponin/ECG/TSH in DRESS; swabs and blood cultures; CXR, SpO2; biopsy + DIF, frozen section vs SSSS; HIV, Mycoplasma; **no rechallenge or prick test**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - SCORTEN, RegiSCAR, ALDEN`,`**SCORTEN** day 1 and day 3: age 40+, malignancy, HR 120+, detachment over 10%, urea over 10 mmol/L, bicarbonate under 20, glucose over 14 mmol/L; mortality 0-1 3%, 2 12%, 3 35%, 4 58%, **5+ about 90%**; DRESS - RegiSCAR 6+ definite; causality - **ALDEN**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Stop culprit and all non-essential drugs immediately**; burns unit/ICU if detachment over 10% or **SCORTEN 2+**; warm room 25-28 C; **leave detached epidermis**, paraffin, non-adherent dressings; **avoid silver sulfadiazine**; fluids **2 mL/kg/% BSA/24 h** to urine 0.5-1 mL/kg/h; NG feeding.`],[`Treatment - immunomodulation`,`**Ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day 7-10 days** (most consistent benefit); **etanercept 50 mg SC single dose** (25 mg under 65 kg); early short steroid (dexamethasone 0.1-0.3 mg/kg/day, within 72 h, 3-5 days) in Indian units; **IVIG alone not shown to help**.`],[`Treatment - supportive care`,`Daily ophthalmology - **preservative-free lubricants hourly, moxifloxacin, topical steroid, glass-rod lysis**; chlorhexidine mouthwash, lidocaine; paracetamol and opioid - **no NSAIDs**; enoxaparin 40 mg, PPI; **antibiotics only for proven infection**.`],[`Treatment - DRESS, AGEP, FDE`,`DRESS with organ involvement - **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg tapered over 6-12 weeks or more**; mild - potent topical steroid; AGEP - withdraw drug, topical steroid, **no antibiotics**; FDE - topical steroid.`],[`Special groups`,`ATT rash (non-severe) - stop all, reintroduce **one at a time every 3 days (INH, rifampicin, ethambutol, PZA last)**; never after SJS/DRESS; pregnancy - specialist team; renal - ciclosporin 3 mg/kg.`],[`Complications`,`Sepsis (leading cause of death), respiratory failure, **ocular sequelae (symblepharon, dry eye, blindness)**, strictures, nail loss, dyspigmentation; DRESS - hepatitis, myocarditis, **late thyroiditis and type 1 diabetes**.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Skin pain out of proportion, dusky erythema, Nikolsky, 2+ mucosae, fever over 38.5 C, facial oedema with eosinophilia, red eye, stridor/hypoxia, falling urine output - same-day admission.`],[`Follow-up and prevention`,`**Lifelong avoidance of culprit and cross-reacting class** with alert card; report to **PvPI 1800-180-3024**; eye follow-up; DRESS TSH and glucose at 3, 6, 12 months; HLA testing before carbamazepine/allopurinol where available; start allopurinol 100 mg.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Withdraw the drug first; count only detached or Nikolsky-positive skin; SCORTEN 2+ = ICU; aromatic anticonvulsants cross-react - use levetiracetam or valproate; FDE recurs at the same site with slate-grey pigment.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Drug rash triage in OPD`,steps:[{label:`Drug chart: what started 4 days-8 weeks ago?`,detail:`Include OTC, ayurvedic, injections`},{label:`Skin pain, Nikolsky, 2+ mucosae, blisters?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`SJS/TEN - stop drug, admit, SCORTEN`},{label:`Facial oedema, fever, nodes, eosinophilia?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`DRESS - LFT, renal, troponin; prednisolone if organ involved`},{label:`Pinhead pustules 1-4 days after drug?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`AGEP - withdraw, topical steroid, no antibiotics`},{label:`Simple exanthem, well patient`,tone:`good`,detail:`Stop drug, antihistamine, topical steroid; review 1 week`},{label:`Report to PvPI, drug alert card`,tone:`good`,detail:`Name the whole cross-reacting class`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Managing TEN - in order`,steps:[{label:`Stop culprit and non-essential drugs`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Single most important act`},{label:`Airway, IV through intact skin, catheter`,detail:`Early intubation if stridor or hypoxia`},{label:`Fluids and warmth`,detail:`2 mL/kg/% BSA/24 h; urine 0.5-1 mL/kg/h; room 25-28 C`},{label:`Skin, eye, mouth care`,detail:`Leave epidermis in place; no silver sulfadiazine; daily ophthalmology`},{label:`Immunomodulator early`,detail:`Ciclosporin 3-5 mg/kg/day 7-10 days or etanercept 50 mg once`},{label:`SCORTEN day 1 and 3; cultures`,tone:`good`,detail:`Antibiotics only for proven infection`}]}]},"dermatology-sti-genital-ulcer-urethral-discharge":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and classification`,points:[`NACO estimates about **3-3.5 crore STI and RTI episodes** a year, managed through **Suraksha clinics** and targeted interventions with free kits.`,`An STI raises the risk of acquiring HIV **two- to five-fold**.`,`**Herpes** is now the commonest cause of genital ulcer in India.`,`India uses **syndromic case management** with colour-coded kits: urethral discharge, genital ulcer, vaginal discharge, lower abdominal pain, bubo, scrotal swelling.`,`Genital ulcer causes: **herpes, syphilis, chancroid, LGV, donovanosis**.`,`Urethritis is **gonococcal** (profuse pus, 2-5 days) or **non-gonococcal** (scanty, 1-3 weeks).`,`Syphilis stages: primary, secondary, early latent, late latent, tertiary; neurosyphilis at any stage.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Treponema pallidum** divides slowly (30-33 hours), so treatment needs long penicillin levels.`,`Endarteritis with plasma cells makes the chancre **indurated, clean and painless**.`,`**Haemophilus ducreyi** toxin gives a soft, painful, undermined ulcer.`,`**Herpes** becomes latent in sacral ganglia, so it recurs and is never cured.`,`Most herpes spreads through **asymptomatic shedding** without visible lesions.`,`Chlamydia provokes little inflammation, so **most infections are silent**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Take a private sexual history using the **5 Ps**: partners, practices, protection, past STI, pregnancy.`,`Ask the date of last contact and symptom onset to estimate incubation.`,`Ask whether the ulcer is **painful** and whether it began as **blisters**.`,`Recurrent ulcers at one site suggest herpes or **fixed drug eruption**.`,`Sudden severe testicular pain is **torsion** until proved otherwise.`,`Ask about HIV status, **penicillin allergy** and the partner's pregnancy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine with consent, privacy and a chaperone; retract the prepuce.`,`Describe the ulcer: number, edge, base, **induration and tenderness**.`,`Nodes: rubbery bilateral (syphilis), suppurating bubo (chancroid), **groove sign** (LGV).`,`**Milk the urethra** after 2-4 hours without voiding if no discharge is visible.`,`Look at palms, soles and mouth for **secondary syphilis**.`,`Look for warts, molluscum, scabies and signs of HIV.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Gram stain: **Gram-negative intracellular diplococci** mean gonorrhoea.`,`**RPR or VDRL plus TPHA** for every STI patient.`,`RPR alone reactive suggests a **biological false positive**; TPHA stays positive for life.`,`Record the **quantitative RPR titre** before treatment.`,`Offer **HIV, HBsAg and anti-HCV** testing to every patient.`,`Biopsy any ulcer not healed after **4-6 weeks** of correct treatment.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Kit 1 (grey)** for discharge: cefixime 400 mg plus azithromycin 1 g, single dose.`,`WHO 2024 prefers **ceftriaxone 1 g IM** for gonorrhoea and doxycycline 7 days for chlamydia.`,`**Kit 3 (white)** for non-herpetic ulcer: benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM plus azithromycin 1 g.`,`**Kit 5 (red)** for herpes: aciclovir 400 mg three times daily for 7 days.`,`**Kit 4 (blue)** for penicillin allergy: doxycycline 15 days plus azithromycin.`,`**Kit 7 (black)** for bubo: doxycycline 21 days plus azithromycin 1 g.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Early syphilis**: benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM once; late latent weekly for 3 weeks.`,`Keep **adrenaline ready** during penicillin injection.`,`**Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction** within 2-24 hours is not allergy - give paracetamol.`,`**Neurosyphilis**: aqueous penicillin 18-24 MU a day IV for 10-14 days.`,`Herpes suppression with aciclovir 400 mg twice daily for 6 or more recurrences a year.`,`Persistent urethritis: metronidazole 2 g, then cover Mycoplasma genitalium.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Syphilis in pregnancy** is treated the same day with penicillin; desensitise if allergic.`,`Doxycycline is **contraindicated in pregnancy**; use azithromycin instead.`,`Herpes near term: **aciclovir from 36 weeks**; caesarean if lesions in labour.`,`Congenital syphilis: aqueous penicillin 50,000 units/kg IV for 10 days.`,`**STI in a child** is abuse until proved otherwise - report under **POCSO**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`The **4 Cs**: compliance, condoms, counselling, contact tracing.`,`Treat partners of the last **60 days (discharge)** or **90 days (ulcer)**.`,`No sex until 7 days after both partners finish treatment.`,`Offer hepatitis B vaccine, **PrEP** for high risk, and HIV PEP within 72 hours.`,`Universal antenatal syphilis screening under **EVTHS**.`,`Confidentiality is protected under the **HIV and AIDS Act 2017**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review all syndromic cases at **7 days**.`,`RPR at 6 and 12 months; a **fourfold fall** means cure.`,`Retest for gonorrhoea and chlamydia at **3 months**.`,`Refer torsion to surgery urgently; **aspirate a bubo**, never incise it.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for genital ulcer and urethral discharge`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`2.4 MU deep IM (1.2 MU each buttock)`,`Once; weekly x 3 for late latent`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer, rare anaphylaxis`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1 g IM single dose (WHO 2024)`,`Once`,`Injection pain; dilute in lidocaine`],[`Cefixime`,`400 mg orally`,`Single dose`,`Diarrhoea, rash`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g orally, empty stomach`,`Single dose`,`Nausea, QT prolongation`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg twice daily orally`,`7 days chlamydia; 21 days LGV`,`Oesophagitis, photosensitivity; not pregnancy`],[`Aciclovir`,`400 mg three times daily orally`,`7-10 days first; 5 days recurrence`,`Reduce in renal impairment`],[`Metronidazole`,`2 g orally after food`,`Single dose`,`No alcohol for 48 hours`],[`Aqueous penicillin G`,`18-24 MU a day IV`,`10-14 days (neurosyphilis)`,`Specialist care`]]},{heading:`Genital ulcers compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Syphilis`,`Chancroid`,`Herpes`,`LGV`,`Donovanosis`],rows:[[`Ulcer`,`Single, clean, indurated`,`Soft, ragged, undermined`,`Grouped vesicles, erosions`,`Transient papule`,`Beefy-red, bleeds`],[`Pain`,`Painless`,`Painful`,`Painful`,`Painless`,`Painless`],[`Nodes`,`Bilateral rubbery`,`Unilateral suppurating bubo`,`Bilateral tender`,`Groove sign`,`Pseudobubo`],[`Confirm`,`Dark-ground, RPR plus TPHA`,`Gram stain, culture`,`Tzanck, HSV PCR`,`NAAT L1-L3`,`Donovan bodies`]]},{heading:`NACO colour-coded kits`,columns:[`Kit and colour`,`Syndrome`,`Contents`],rows:[[`Kit 1 Grey`,`Urethral or cervical discharge`,`Cefixime 400 mg plus azithromycin 1 g`],[`Kit 2 Green`,`Vaginitis`,`Secnidazole 2 g plus fluconazole 150 mg`],[`Kit 3 White`,`Non-herpetic genital ulcer`,`Benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU plus azithromycin 1 g`],[`Kit 4 Blue`,`Ulcer with penicillin allergy`,`Doxycycline 15 days plus azithromycin 1 g`],[`Kit 5 Red`,`Herpetic ulcer`,`Aciclovir 400 mg three times daily, 7 days`],[`Kit 6 Yellow`,`Lower abdominal pain (PID)`,`Cefixime, metronidazole and doxycycline 14 days`],[`Kit 7 Black`,`Inguinal bubo`,`Doxycycline 21 days plus azithromycin 1 g`]]}],remember:[`Herpes is now the commonest genital ulcer in India.`,`Painless indurated ulcer is syphilis; painful soft undermined ulcer is chancroid.`,`Groove sign means LGV; bleeding beefy-red ulcer means donovanosis.`,`RPR for screening and follow-up; TPHA confirms and stays positive for life.`,`One benzathine injection cures early syphilis; three weekly for late latent.`,`Kits: grey discharge, white ulcer, blue allergy, red herpes, yellow PID, black bubo.`,`The 4 Cs: compliance, condoms, counselling, contact tracing.`]},updated:[`WHO 2026: first recommendation on doxycycline PEP (200 mg within 72 h of condomless sex) for MSM and transgender women, prioritising recent or recurrent STIs, with AMR monitoring; not yet NACO programme policy`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Genital ulcer and urethral discharge`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`STI = infection spread mainly by sexual contact; about **3-3.5 crore STI/RTI episodes/year** in India; STI raises HIV acquisition **2-5 fold**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Syndromic (NACO)**: urethral discharge, genital ulcer (herpetic/non-herpetic), vaginal discharge, lower abdominal pain, bubo, scrotal swelling, anorectal discharge - each a **colour-coded kit**; syphilis stages primary, secondary, early latent, late latent, tertiary.`],[`Aetiology`,`Ulcer: **HSV (commonest now)**, syphilis, chancroid, LGV, donovanosis; discharge: **gonococcal** (2-5 days, profuse pus) vs **NGU** (chlamydia, M. genitalium, Trichomonas - scanty, 1-3 weeks).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`T. pallidum -> **endarteritis obliterans** -> indurated painless chancre -> haematogenous secondary at 6-12 weeks; slow division -> needs depot penicillin; HSV latency in sacral ganglia -> recurrence; gonococcal pili -> neutrophil pus; ulcers recruit CD4 cells -> HIV.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Private confidential **5 Ps** (partners, practices, protection, past STI, pregnancy); last contact and onset; painful vs painless, vesicles first; recurrence (herpes/FDE); discharge amount; scrotal pain (torsion); anal sex; rash, headache, eye/ear symptoms; penicillin allergy; child - POCSO.`],[`Key examination`,`Chaperone; ulcer number, edge, base, **induration, tenderness**; nodes - rubbery bilateral (syphilis), **suppurating unilateral bubo (chancroid)**, **groove sign (LGV)**, pseudobubo (donovanosis); milk urethra; testes; palms/soles rash, condylomata lata; HIV signs.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**Gram stain: Gram-negative intracellular diplococci**; 2+ polymorphs/oil field without GNID = NGU; **RPR/VDRL + TPHA** (quantitative titre before treatment; prozone, window period); **HIV, HBsAg, anti-HCV**; NAAT first-void urine; DGM, Tzanck/HSV PCR; biopsy if not healed in 4-6 weeks.`],[`Treatment - discharge`,`**Kit 1 grey: cefixime 400 mg + azithromycin 1 g stat**; **WHO 2024: ceftriaxone 1 g IM** for gonorrhoea (CDC 500 mg), **doxycycline 100 mg BD 7 days** for chlamydia; persistent - metronidazole 2 g, then doxycycline + moxifloxacin for M. genitalium.`],[`Treatment - ulcer`,`**Kit 3 white: benzathine penicillin 2.4 MU IM + azithromycin 1 g**; **Kit 4 blue** (penicillin allergy): doxycycline 100 mg BD 15 days + azithromycin 1 g; **Kit 5 red: aciclovir 400 mg TDS 7 days**; **Kit 7 black** (bubo/LGV): doxycycline BD 21 days + azithromycin 1 g.`],[`Treatment - syphilis by stage`,`Early: **BPG 2.4 MU IM once**; late latent/unknown: **2.4 MU weekly x 3**; neurosyphilis/ocular/otic: **aqueous penicillin 18-24 MU/day IV 10-14 days**; warn of **Jarisch-Herxheimer** reaction; adrenaline ready.`],[`Treatment - genital herpes`,`First episode aciclovir 400 mg TDS 7-10 days or valaciclovir 1 g BD; recurrence 400 mg TDS 5 days from prodrome; **suppression aciclovir 400 mg BD or valaciclovir 500 mg OD** for 6+ recurrences/year; HIV - 400 mg five times daily.`],[`Special groups`,`**Syphilis in pregnancy - penicillin the same day** (desensitise if allergic); EVTHS universal screening; congenital syphilis - aqueous penicillin 50,000 U/kg IV 12-hourly then 8-hourly to 10 days; pregnancy chlamydia - azithromycin; HSV in third trimester - aciclovir from 36 weeks.`],[`Prevention`,`**4 Cs - compliance, condoms, counselling, contact tracing** (60 days discharge, 90 days ulcer); HBV vaccine; oral PrEP; **HIV PEP TLD 28 days within 72 h**; **doxy-PEP 200 mg within 72 h - WHO 2026** for MSM/TGW with recent STI (not NACO policy yet).`],[`Complications`,`Epididymo-orchitis, PID, infertility, ectopic, reactive arthritis, DGI, neurosyphilis, congenital syphilis, LGV strictures and esthiomene, HIV acquisition.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`STI in a child (POCSO), acute scrotum with high-riding testis (torsion), neuro/ocular signs (neurosyphilis), DGI, non-healing ulcer, reactive serology in pregnancy, LGV proctitis in MSM.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review syndromic cases at **7 days**; quantitative RPR at **6 and 12 months** (fourfold fall = cure); retest gonorrhoea/chlamydia at 3 months; link HIV-positive to ART centre; record in SIMS.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO May 2026: first doxy-PEP recommendation**; WHO 2024 - ceftriaxone 1 g IM for gonorrhoea, doxycycline preferred for chlamydia; CDC 2021 remains the US standard.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Herpes is now the commonest ulcer; painless indurated = syphilis, painful soft undermined + bubo = chancroid; groove sign = LGV; aspirate a bubo through healthy skin, never incise; every STI is an indication for HIV and syphilis testing.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Genital ulcer - syndromic algorithm (NACO)`,steps:[{label:`Grouped vesicles or recurrent painful erosions?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Yes - Kit 5 red: aciclovir 400 mg TDS 7 days`},{label:`Non-herpetic ulcer`,detail:`Kit 3 white: BPG 2.4 MU IM + azithromycin 1 g`},{label:`Penicillin allergy?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Kit 4 blue: doxycycline 100 mg BD 15 days + azithromycin 1 g`},{label:`Fluctuant bubo or groove sign?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Kit 7 black: doxycycline 21 days + azithromycin; aspirate, never incise`},{label:`RPR/TPHA, HIV, HBV, HCV; 4 Cs`,detail:`Treat partners of last 90 days`},{label:`Review at 7 days; biopsy if not healed 4-6 weeks`,tone:`good`,detail:`RPR at 6 and 12 months`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Five genital ulcers at a glance`,columns:[`Disease`,`Ulcer`,`Nodes`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Primary syphilis`,`Single, clean, indurated, painless`,`Bilateral rubbery, non-tender`,`BPG 2.4 MU IM once`],[`Chancroid`,`Multiple, soft, undermined, painful`,`Unilateral suppurating bubo`,`Azithromycin 1 g once`],[`Herpes`,`Grouped vesicles, shallow painful erosions`,`Bilateral tender`,`Aciclovir 400 mg TDS 7-10 days`],[`LGV`,`Transient painless papule`,`Matted, groove sign`,`Doxycycline 100 mg BD 21 days`],[`Donovanosis`,`Beefy-red, bleeds on touch, painless`,`Pseudobubo`,`Azithromycin 1 g weekly 3+ weeks`]]}]},"dermatology-superficial-fungal-infections":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Superficial mycoses infect only **stratum corneum, hair and nails**.`,`Three groups: **dermatophytes** (tinea), **Candida** and **Malassezia**.`,`India's epidemic strain is **Trichophyton indotineae**, often terbinafine-resistant.`,`Drivers: **steroid-antifungal combination creams**, under-dosing and family spread.`,`Topical steroids have been **Schedule H** since 2018 - not to be sold over the counter.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`By site: tinea corporis, cruris, pedis, manuum, faciei, capitis, barbae and unguium.`,`**Tinea incognito** is steroid-modified tinea without a clear edge.`,`**Chronic** lasts over 6 months to 1 year; **recurrent** relapses within weeks of cure.`,`**Recalcitrant** means no response despite adequate dose and duration.`,`By source: anthropophilic, **zoophilic** (more inflammatory) and geophilic.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Dermatophytes digest keratin and cannot grow at 37 C in living tissue.`,`Hyphae grow outwards, so the **active edge** is scaly and the centre clears.`,`**Topical steroids** suppress local immunity, so tinea spreads without an edge.`,`Heat, humidity, sweat and tight synthetic clothing favour infection.`,`**Terbinafine** blocks squalene epoxidase; T. indotineae carries **SQLE mutations**.`,`**Itraconazole** concentrates in keratin but needs **acid and a fatty meal** to absorb.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask exactly **which creams** were used and ask to see the tubes.`,`Ask previous oral antifungal: drug, dose, brand and duration.`,`Ask whether **family members or roommates itch**.`,`Ask about diabetes, **SGLT2 inhibitors**, HIV, steroids and obesity.`,`In a child with scalp patches, ask about **pets** and siblings.`,`Check liver disease, heart failure and interacting drugs before azoles.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine whole skin, scalp, nails, feet, groin and buttocks.`,`Tinea corporis: annular plaque with **raised scaly active edge** and central clearing.`,`**Tinea cruris spares the scrotum**; scrotal involvement with satellite pustules is Candida.`,`Tinea incognito: no clear edge, pustules, atrophy and striae.`,`Tinea capitis: black dots, scaly patches or a boggy **kerion** - never incise it.`,`Pityriasis versicolor: fine-scaly macules on upper trunk; **evoked scale sign**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**KOH mount is mandatory** before systemic therapy - 10% for skin, 20-40% for nails.`,`Scrape the active edge; stop topical antifungals for 1 week before.`,`**Spaghetti and meatballs** on KOH means pityriasis versicolor.`,`Wood's lamp: green in Microsporum, yellow-orange in PV; T. tonsurans does not fluoresce.`,`Culture and susceptibility for chronic, recurrent or failed cases.`,`Check **blood glucose** in extensive tinea and HIV when atypical.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Stop all steroid-containing creams** and explain why.`,`Topical azole or terbinafine 1% to lesion plus **2 cm margin**, twice daily.`,`Continue **2-4 weeks after the skin looks clear**.`,`Treat **all affected family members together**; hot-wash clothes and towels.`,`Loose cotton clothes, dry folds, separate towels.`,`Levocetirizine at night for itch so patients do not return to steroids.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Extensive or chronic tinea: **itraconazole 200 mg daily** for 4-8 weeks, plus topical.`,`Take itraconazole right after a fatty meal; **avoid PPIs and antacids**.`,`**Terbinafine 250 mg** once daily (often twice daily in India) for 4-6 weeks.`,`No response after 2-4 weeks of full-dose terbinafine - **switch to itraconazole**.`,`Onychomycosis: terbinafine 6 weeks (fingers) or 12 weeks (toes), or itraconazole pulses.`,`PV: **ketoconazole 2% shampoo** daily for 5 days; oral terbinafine does not work.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Tinea capitis**: griseofulvin 20-25 mg/kg/day for 6-8 weeks for Microsporum.`,`Terbinafine for 4 weeks by weight is best for **Trichophyton** capitis.`,`Add ketoconazole shampoo twice weekly for the child and family.`,`**Pregnancy**: topical clotrimazole or miconazole only; defer nail treatment.`,`Itraconazole is **contraindicated in heart failure**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review at **2-4 weeks**; the end point is clinical cure plus 2-4 weeks.`,`Check LFT if therapy exceeds 6 weeks or with liver disease or alcohol.`,`Refer if no response after 4 weeks of full-dose itraconazole.`,`Toenails take **12-18 months** to grow out - judge by new clear nail.`,`At each recurrence recheck steroid use, adherence, contacts and sugar.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for superficial fungal infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Clotrimazole 1% or luliconazole 1%`,`Topical twice daily (luliconazole once)`,`2-4 weeks after clearance`,`Local irritation`],[`Itraconazole`,`100 mg twice daily or 200 mg daily, after fatty meal`,`4-8 weeks`,`Hepatitis; heart failure; CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Terbinafine`,`250 mg once (or twice) daily`,`4-6 weeks; toenails 12 weeks`,`Taste loss, hepatotoxicity`],[`Griseofulvin`,`20-25 mg/kg/day (microsize) with milk`,`6-8 weeks (capitis)`,`Photosensitivity; not in pregnancy`],[`Fluconazole`,`150-300 mg once weekly`,`2-6 weeks`,`Choice for Candida; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Ketoconazole 2% shampoo`,`Leave 5 minutes, then rinse`,`PV daily for 5 days`,`Scalp dryness`],[`Amorolfine 5% nail lacquer`,`Once weekly after filing`,`6-12 months`,`Adjunct to oral therapy`],[`Levocetirizine`,`5 mg at night`,`While itching`,`Drowsiness`]]},{heading:`Types of dermatophytosis and treatment`,columns:[`Type`,`Key feature`,`First-line treatment`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Corporis or cruris, limited`,`Annular plaque, scrotum spared`,`Topical azole or terbinafine`,`Clear plus 2-4 weeks`],[`Corporis or cruris, extensive`,`Multisite, recurrent, steroid-modified`,`Itraconazole plus topical`,`4-8 weeks`],[`Capitis`,`Black dots, kerion; children`,`Griseofulvin or terbinafine plus shampoo`,`6-8 weeks or 4 weeks`],[`Onychomycosis`,`Thick nail, subungual debris`,`Terbinafine or itraconazole pulses plus lacquer`,`Fingers 6, toes 12 weeks`],[`Pedis`,`Fourth web maceration, moccasin`,`Topical terbinafine; oral if moccasin`,`2-6 weeks`]]},{heading:`Hypopigmented patch - fungus or not`,columns:[`Feature`,`Pityriasis versicolor`,`Vitiligo`,`Leprosy`],rows:[[`Colour`,`Hypo or hyperpigmented`,`Milky chalk-white`,`Hypopigmented, never depigmented`],[`Surface`,`Fine scale`,`No scale`,`Dry, hairless`],[`Sensation`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Lost`],[`Key test`,`KOH spaghetti and meatballs`,`Wood's lamp chalk-white`,`Sensation, nerves, slit-skin smear`]]}],remember:[`No systemic antifungal without a KOH.`,`Tinea cruris spares the scrotum; Candida does not.`,`Never prescribe a steroid-antifungal combination cream.`,`Apply 2 cm beyond the edge; continue 2-4 weeks after clearance.`,`Itraconazole needs food and acid - no PPIs.`,`Griseofulvin for Microsporum, terbinafine for Trichophyton capitis.`,`Nails: fingers 6 weeks or 2 pulses, toes 12 weeks or 3 pulses.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Superficial fungal infections`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Fungal infection confined to **stratum corneum, hair and nails** - dermatophytes (tinea), Candida, Malassezia; India has an epidemic of chronic, recurrent, steroid-modified tinea.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By site: corporis, cruris, pedis, manuum, faciei, capitis, barbae, unguium, **incognito**; **chronic (over 6 months-1 year), recurrent, recalcitrant**; anthropophilic, zoophilic, geophilic species.`],[`Aetiology and risk factors`,`**Trichophyton indotineae** (terbinafine-resistant SQLE mutations), **steroid-antifungal-antibiotic combination creams**, under-dosing, heat, humidity, tight synthetic clothes, sharing, diabetes, obesity, HIV, **SGLT2 inhibitors** (Candida).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Arthroconidia adhere -> keratinases -> hyphae spread centrifugally -> **active scaly edge with central clearing**; steroid suppresses CMI -> incognito, pseudo-imbricata; terbinafine blocks squalene epoxidase (fungicidal); azoles block CYP51 (fungistatic).`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Duration, spread, **every cream used (see the tubes)**, previous oral antifungal - drug, dose, brand, duration; family members itching; hygiene, clothing; diabetes, SGLT2i, HIV, steroids; liver disease, interacting drugs; pregnancy; numbness (leprosy).`],[`Key examination`,`Whole skin, nails, feet, groin, scalp; annular raised scaly edge; **cruris spares scrotum** (Candida involves it, satellite pustules); moccasin feet; kerion (never incise); onychomycosis types; PV fine scale; steroid atrophy and striae.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**KOH 10% skin, 20-40% nails from active edge** before systemic therapy (stop topicals 1 week); spaghetti and meatballs (PV); Wood's lamp green (Microsporum), yellow-orange (PV); culture and susceptibility for chronic/failure; nail PAS; glucose/HbA1c, HIV if atypical.`],[`Treatment - general`,`**Stop all steroid creams** (Schedule H since 2018); daily bath, dry folds, loose cotton; hot-wash clothes; **treat whole family together**; continue **2-4 weeks after clearance**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Limited, naive: **topical azole (clotrimazole 1% BD, luliconazole 1% OD) or terbinafine 1% BD**, lesion + 2 cm margin, until 2-4 weeks after clearance (4-6 weeks total).`],[`Escalation / systemic`,`Extensive/chronic: **itraconazole 100 mg BD or 200 mg OD 4-8 weeks, plus 2-4 weeks after cure**, right after a fatty meal, no PPI (SUBA-itraconazole avoids this); terbinafine 250 mg OD (often BD in India) - **switch to itraconazole if no response in 2-4 weeks**; add topical of another class.`],[`Treatment - specific sites`,`**Capitis**: griseofulvin 20-25 mg/kg/day 6-8 weeks (Microsporum) or terbinafine by weight 4 weeks (Trichophyton) + ketoconazole shampoo; **nails**: terbinafine 6 weeks fingers/12 weeks toes or itraconazole pulses 2/3 + amorolfine lacquer; **PV**: ketoconazole 2% shampoo 5 days, fluconazole 300 mg weekly x 2 - oral terbinafine fails; **Candida**: clotrimazole/miconazole 2 weeks, fluconazole 150 mg weekly.`],[`Drug cautions`,`Itraconazole - **contraindicated in heart failure**, CYP3A4 inhibitor (simvastatin, midazolam); terbinafine - taste loss, hepatotoxicity, rare SJS, CYP2D6; griseofulvin - with milk, enzyme inducer, not in pregnancy, men avoid fathering for 6 months.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy/lactation**: topical clotrimazole or miconazole only, defer nail treatment; children: topical azoles, terbinafine by weight band, griseofulvin for Microsporum; diabetes: glucose control.`],[`Complications`,`Tinea incognito, steroid atrophy/striae, kerion with scarring alopecia, secondary bacterial infection, chronic recurrence, drug hepatotoxicity.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`No response after 4 weeks of full-dose itraconazole (culture, susceptibility), kerion or scarring alopecia, jaundice on antifungal (stop, LFT), mucosal rash (SJS), anaesthetic patch (leprosy), proximal subungual onychomycosis (HIV).`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **2-4 weeks**; end point clinical cure + 2-4 weeks (negative KOH in chronic); LFT if course over 6 weeks or liver disease; toenails take 12-18 months; recheck steroid use, adherence, contacts, sugar at every recurrence.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Scrape the edge; no systemic antifungal without KOH; never a steroid-combination cream; apply 2 cm beyond the edge; itraconazole needs food and acid; T. indotineae resists terbinafine - switch to itraconazole.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Annular scaly itchy rash - tinea work-up`,steps:[{label:`Steroid or combination cream used?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Stop it; expect incognito pattern and flare`},{label:`KOH from active edge`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Septate hyphae confirm; negative may still be treated if typical`},{label:`Limited and treatment-naive?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Topical azole or terbinafine to 2-4 weeks after clearance`},{label:`Extensive, chronic or recurrent`,detail:`Itraconazole 200 mg/day 4-8 weeks + topical of another class`},{label:`No response at 4 weeks of full dose`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Culture, susceptibility, dermatology referral`},{label:`Family treated, hygiene, sugar checked`,tone:`good`,detail:`Cure plus 2-4 weeks before stopping`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Systemic antifungal - which and when`,columns:[`Drug`,`Best for`,`Dose`,`Key caution`],rows:[[`Itraconazole`,`Chronic Indian tinea, nails (pulse)`,`200 mg/day 4-8 weeks; 200 mg BD 1 week/month`,`Fatty meal, no PPI; heart failure`],[`Terbinafine`,`Trichophyton, onychomycosis, capitis`,`250 mg/day; toenails 12 weeks`,`Resistance (SQLE); liver; taste loss`],[`Griseofulvin`,`Microsporum tinea capitis`,`20-25 mg/kg/day 6-8 weeks`,`With milk; not in pregnancy`],[`Fluconazole`,`Candida, PV, children with interactions`,`150-300 mg weekly`,`Weak for dermatophytes; avoid in pregnancy`]]}]},"dermatology-generalised-pruritus":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Pruritus** is an unpleasant sensation that provokes the desire to scratch.`,`**Chronic pruritus** lasts **6 weeks or more**.`,`**Xerosis** is the commonest cause in people over 65.`,`Lymphoma, polycythaemia and cholestasis can itch **months to years** before diagnosis.`,`Generalised itch on normal skin is **systemic until the blood panel says otherwise**.`]},{title:`Classification and causes`,points:[`**IFSI Group I**: itch on inflamed skin with a primary rash; **Group II**: normal-looking skin with only scratch marks.`,`**IFSI Group III**: chronic scratch lesions - prurigo nodularis, lichen simplex chronicus.`,`Cause categories: dermatological, systemic, neurological, psychogenic, mixed, undetermined.`,`Skin causes: **scabies**, eczema, urticaria, xerosis, lichen planus, bullous pemphigoid.`,`Systemic causes: **CKD, cholestasis**, thyroid disease, iron deficiency.`,`Haematological: **polycythaemia vera** and Hodgkin lymphoma; also HIV, opioids, chloroquine and cholestatic drugs.`,`Neuropathic causes: **brachioradial pruritus** and notalgia paraesthetica.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Itch travels in **unmyelinated C fibres** to the dorsal horn and spinothalamic tract.`,`**Histaminergic itch** (urticaria, bites) is the only itch reliably helped by antihistamines.`,`Most chronic itch is **non-histaminergic**, driven by proteases, IL-31, IL-4 and IL-13.`,`Scratching causes pain, which switches off itch - but damages the barrier.`,`Mu-opioid activity causes itch; **kappa agonists** and naltrexone relieve it.`,`Cholestatic itch follows **autotaxin and LPA**, not bile acid levels.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Score severity on a **0-10 scale** to follow response.`,`Family members itching at night means **scabies** until proved otherwise.`,`Itch within minutes of water contact suggests **polycythaemia vera**.`,`Ask every drug of the last 3 months, including ayurvedic remedies.`,`Ask about jaundice, B symptoms, dialysis, thyroid symptoms and menorrhagia.`,`Palm and sole itch in the third trimester suggests **cholestasis of pregnancy**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`First decide: **primary rash, or only scratch changes**.`,`The **butterfly sign** - spared mid-back - proves itch without a primary rash.`,`Look for **burrows** and genital papules of scabies.`,`Look for xerosis with crazy-paving shins and tense blisters in the elderly.`,`Check pallor, jaundice, plethora, nodes, liver, spleen and thyroid.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`With a clear primary rash, **treat it first** - no blood tests needed.`,`Normal skin: **CBC with eosinophils, ESR, ferritin, LFT, creatinine, TSH, glucose**.`,`Add HIV and hepatitis serology; chest X-ray for persistent unexplained itch.`,`High haemoglobin with aquagenic itch - send **JAK2 V617F**.`,`In pregnancy, **bile acids 19 micromol/L or more** confirm ICP.`,`Repeat the panel at **6-12 months** if itch stays unexplained.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Emollients are the foundation**: soak and seal within 3 minutes of bathing.`,`Short lukewarm baths and soap substitutes; avoid hot water and wool.`,`Stop or change any suspected culprit drug.`,`**Treat the cause** - iron, thyroid control, dialysis, phlebotomy, lymphoma treatment.`,`**Menthol 1-2%** in aqueous cream cools and is safe in the elderly.`,`Non-sedating antihistamines help urticaria, **not most chronic itch**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`CKD itch: **gabapentin 100 mg after each dialysis** or difelikefalin IV.`,`Cholestatic ladder: **cholestyramine, then rifampicin, then naltrexone, then sertraline**.`,`SSRIs or **mirtazapine 7.5-15 mg** at night for paraneoplastic and nocturnal itch.`,`Polycythaemia: phlebotomy, **aspirin 75-100 mg**, ruxolitinib if resistant.`,`Capsaicin cream for localised neuropathic itch.`,`Narrowband UVB helps uraemic, HIV and prurigo itch.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**ICP**: palm and sole itch, no rash, third trimester; resolves after delivery.`,`ICP bile acids **100 or more**: consider birth at 35-36 weeks.`,`Ursodeoxycholic acid may ease ICP itch but does not reduce stillbirth.`,`**Elderly**: treat xerosis first; **avoid sedating antihistamines** (Beers criteria).`,`Crusted scabies in institutions: ivermectin plus permethrin, and treat everyone.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review in **2-4 weeks** with itch score, sleep and mood (PHQ-9).`,`Refer to haematology for nodes, B symptoms, splenomegaly or high haemoglobin.`,`Refer elderly patients with urticarial plaques or tense blisters - **bullous pemphigoid**.`,`Refer pregnant women with palm and sole itch the **same week**.`,`Refer delusional infestation and suicidal thoughts to psychiatry.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for chronic itch`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Emollient`,`Liberally twice daily, after bath`,`Long term`,`Folliculitis`],[`Menthol 1-2% aqueous cream`,`Topical 2-3 times daily`,`As needed`,`Stings broken skin`],[`Cetirizine or fexofenadine`,`10 mg or 180 mg once daily`,`While histaminergic itch`,`Little help in chronic itch`],[`Gabapentin`,`100 mg after each dialysis; 100-300 mg at night`,`Titrate`,`Dizziness, falls; adjust to eGFR`],[`Cholestyramine`,`4 g once or twice daily, max 16 g`,`Ongoing`,`Bloating; other drugs 4 h apart`],[`Rifampicin`,`150-300 mg daily orally`,`LFT at 2, 6, 12 weeks`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions, interactions`],[`Naltrexone`,`12.5 mg rising to 50 mg daily`,`Ongoing`,`Withdrawal-like reaction; not with opioids`],[`Sertraline`,`50-100 mg once daily`,`Ongoing`,`Hyponatraemia in elderly`],[`Mirtazapine`,`7.5-15 mg at night`,`Ongoing`,`Weight gain, sedation`]]},{heading:`Systemic causes - clue and first test`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`First test`],rows:[[`CKD`,`Worse at night or around dialysis`,`Creatinine, calcium, phosphate, PTH`],[`Cholestasis`,`Palms and soles first, jaundice`,`LFT with ALP and GGT, ultrasound`],[`Iron deficiency`,`Pallor, koilonychia, menorrhagia`,`CBC, ferritin`],[`Thyroid disease`,`Weight change, tremor or dry skin`,`TSH`],[`Polycythaemia vera`,`Itch after water, plethora, spleen`,`CBC, JAK2 V617F`],[`Hodgkin lymphoma`,`Nodes, night sweats, weight loss`,`CBC, ESR, chest X-ray, node biopsy`],[`Cholestasis of pregnancy`,`Third trimester palms and soles`,`Bile acids, LFT`]]},{heading:`IFSI groups of chronic pruritus`,columns:[`Group`,`Skin appearance`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Group I`,`Inflamed skin, primary rash`,`Scabies, eczema, urticaria, pemphigoid`],[`Group II`,`Normal skin, scratch marks only`,`CKD, cholestasis, thyroid, iron, lymphoma`],[`Group III`,`Chronic scratch lesions`,`Prurigo nodularis, lichen simplex chronicus`]]}],remember:[`Chronic itch is 6 weeks or more; classify by IFSI group first.`,`Itch on normal skin is systemic until the blood panel says otherwise.`,`Family itching at night means scabies.`,`Butterfly sign means no primary rash.`,`Cholestatic ladder: cholestyramine, rifampicin, naltrexone, sertraline.`,`Aquagenic itch means check JAK2.`,`Iron deficiency can itch without anaemia.`,`In the elderly: emollient first, no sedating antihistamines.`]},updated:[`International Urticaria Guideline 2026: antihistamine up-dosing in urticaria capped at 4 times the standard dose (citation updated from the 2021/2022 edition)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Generalised pruritus`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Unpleasant sensation provoking the desire to scratch; **chronic = 6 weeks or more**; burden comparable to chronic pain.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**IFSI: Group I inflamed skin, Group II normal-looking skin, Group III chronic scratch lesions** (prurigo nodularis, LSC); aetiology - dermatological, systemic, neurological, somatoform, mixed.`],[`Aetiology`,`Skin: scabies, eczema, urticaria, **xerosis (commonest in elderly)**, pemphigoid; systemic: **CKD, cholestasis/PBC, thyroid, iron deficiency, polycythaemia vera, Hodgkin lymphoma, HIV, drugs (opioids, chloroquine)**; neuropathic: brachioradial, notalgia.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`C fibres -> dorsal horn GRP interneurons -> spinothalamic; **histaminergic (H1) only in urticaria/bites**; most chronic itch non-histaminergic - **PAR-2, IL-31, IL-4/13, TRPV1/TRPA1**; mu-opioid causes itch, kappa suppresses; cholestatic itch - **autotaxin/LPA**; PV - JAK2 mast cells.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Onset, duration, site, night pattern, NRS 0-10; **family members itching (scabies)**; **aquagenic itch**; drugs in last 3 months; jaundice, B symptoms, CKD/dialysis, thyroid, menorrhagia/pica; pregnancy (palms/soles); HIV risk; mood, delusional infestation.`],[`Key examination`,`Primary lesion or only scratch marks; **butterfly sign** (spared mid-back); burrows; xerosis/crazy paving; pemphigoid in elderly; pallor, koilonychia, jaundice, plethora, **splenomegaly, nodes**, thyroid, uraemic features, thrush.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`Primary rash - treat it first; **Group II panel: CBC + eosinophils, smear, ESR, ferritin, LFT (ALP, GGT), urea/creatinine, TSH, glucose/HbA1c, HIV**, hepatitis serology; CXR if persistent; stool ova if eosinophilia; JAK2 if high Hb; AMA if cholestatic; **repeat 6-12 months**.`],[`Treatment - general`,`**Emollients twice daily, soak and seal**; short lukewarm baths, soap substitutes; short nails, cotton; habit reversal; stop culprit drugs; **treat the cause** (iron, thyroid, dialysis, biliary drainage, phlebotomy); sleep hygiene, PHQ-9; NB-UVB.`],[`Treatment - first line drugs`,`**Menthol 1-2% aqueous cream**; topical steroid only for inflamed skin; capsaicin for localised neuropathic itch; **non-sedating antihistamine only for histaminergic itch** (up to 4x dose in urticaria, never more); avoid hydroxyzine in elderly (Beers).`],[`Treatment - CKD-associated pruritus`,`**Gabapentin 100 mg after each dialysis** (or 100-300 mg at night) or pregabalin 25 mg, dose by eGFR; **difelikefalin 0.5 microgram/kg IV after each dialysis** three times weekly; adequate dialysis, phosphate/PTH control.`],[`Treatment - cholestatic itch ladder`,`**Cholestyramine 4-16 g/day** (other drugs 4 h apart) -> **rifampicin 150-300 mg** (LFT at 2, 6, 12 weeks) -> **naltrexone 12.5 to 50 mg** -> **sertraline 75-100 mg** -> hepatology (bezafibrate, IBAT inhibitors, transplant).`],[`Treatment - other causes`,`PV - phlebotomy to Hct under 45%, aspirin 75-100 mg, SSRI or ruxolitinib; lymphoma - treat tumour, **mirtazapine 7.5-15 mg** or paroxetine; iron - ferrous sulphate alternate days; prurigo/atopic itch - **dupilumab, nemolizumab**; delusional infestation - risperidone 0.5-2 mg.`],[`Special groups`,`**ICP**: palm/sole itch, **peak bile acids 19 micromol/L or more**; birth **35-36 weeks if 100 or more**, 38-39 weeks if 40-99; UDCA for itch only (no stillbirth benefit); **elderly**: xerosis first, review drugs, scabies in institutions, pemphigoid, low-dose gabapentin or SSRI.`],[`Complications`,`Excoriation, infection, lichen simplex, **prurigo nodularis**, insomnia, depression, suicidal ideation; missed lymphoma, PV, PBC.`],[`Red flags and referral`,`Weight loss, night sweats, nodes (lymphoma); aquagenic itch with high Hb (PV - JAK2); jaundice with pale stools (obstruction); third-trimester palm/sole itch (bile acids this week); elderly with urticarial plaques or tense blisters (pemphigoid); suicidal thoughts.`],[`Follow-up and monitoring`,`Review **2-4 weeks** with NRS, sleep, mood; sodium at 2-4 weeks on SSRI in elderly; gabapentinoid renal dosing; unexplained itch - **repeat panel and node exam every 6-12 months**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Itch with normal skin is systemic until the panel says otherwise; family itching at night = scabies; antihistamines do little for chronic itch; gabapentin after dialysis; cholestyramine -> rifampicin -> naltrexone -> sertraline; iron deficiency itches without anaemia.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Generalised itch - IFSI approach`,steps:[{label:`Primary rash present? (Group I)`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Treat scabies, eczema, urticaria, xerosis, pemphigoid`},{label:`Household itching at night?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Scabies - treat all contacts the same day`},{label:`Normal skin (Group II) - first-line panel`,detail:`CBC, smear, ESR, ferritin, LFT, creatinine, TSH, glucose, HIV`},{label:`B symptoms, nodes, high Hb, jaundice?`,tone:`warn`,detail:`Lymphoma, polycythaemia vera, cholestasis - refer`},{label:`Treat cause + emollient + mechanism drug`,detail:`Gabapentin (CKD), cholestyramine (cholestasis), SSRI/mirtazapine`},{label:`Unexplained: repeat panel 6-12 months`,tone:`good`,detail:`Lymphoma and PBC can declare later`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Systemic itch: clue, test, drug`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`First test`,`Itch drug`],rows:[[`CKD`,`On dialysis, worse at night`,`Creatinine, PO4, PTH`,`Gabapentin 100 mg post-dialysis; difelikefalin`],[`Cholestasis`,`Palms and soles first`,`ALP, GGT, AMA, USG`,`Cholestyramine, rifampicin, naltrexone`],[`Polycythaemia vera`,`Itch after water contact`,`CBC, JAK2 V617F`,`Aspirin, SSRI, ruxolitinib`],[`Hodgkin lymphoma`,`Nodes, night sweats`,`CBC, ESR, CXR, biopsy`,`Treat tumour; mirtazapine`],[`Iron deficiency`,`Pallor, koilonychia`,`Ferritin`,`Ferrous sulphate`],[`ICP`,`Third trimester palms/soles`,`Bile acids, LFT`,`UDCA, menthol emollient`]]}]}};export{e as default};